<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In 1972, Mumford gave an analytic construction
of degenerations of abelian varieties
over complete rings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.
It played an important role in the development
of the theory of toric varieties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>
and toroidal compactifications of
locally symmetric varieties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
When working over <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the basic idea is that one may view
a degeneration of abelian varieties analytically,
as the quotient of an appropriate intermediate cover
of the universal cover.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is well-known that, over the complex numbers,
any abelian <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-fold
<math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\simeq{\mathbb{C}}^{g}/H_{1}(A,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\simeq{\mathbb{C}}^{g}/H_{1}(A,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient
of a vector space <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by a lattice
<math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(A,{\mathbb{Z}})\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(A,{\mathbb{Z}})\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
of rank <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that
<math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a general fiber of a degenerating
family <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of
abelian varieties over the unit disk
<math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\subset{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\subset{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there is a saturated sublattice
<math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> of
the fundamental group
<math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}=\pi_{1}(X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}=\pi_{1}(X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles
which are invariant under the monodromy
of the punctured family <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}\to\Delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}\to\Delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It contains a further sublattice <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vanishing cycles</span>; that is, the
<math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles on <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> which are null-homologous
in <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
The filtration
<math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset W_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}=V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset W_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}=V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> defines the
<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weight filtration</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The subgroup <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset V_{\mathbb{Z}}=\pi_{1}(X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset V_{\mathbb{Z}}=\pi_{1}(X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>
gives rise to
a cover
<math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{*}\to X^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}\to X^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding fiberwise
to the intermediate cover
<math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{g}\simeq{\widetilde{X}}_{t}\to Y_{t}\to X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>≃</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{g}\simeq{\widetilde{X}}_{t}\to Y_{t}\to X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of the universal cover,
whose Galois group over <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the graded piece <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In general, <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semiabelian variety</span>—an
algebraic torus bundle over an abelian variety
of dimension <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}\textrm{rank}\,{\rm gr}_{-1}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>rank</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}\textrm{rank}\,{\rm gr}_{-1}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
has rank <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>,
then we have
<math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\simeq{\mathbb{C}}^{g}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\simeq{\mathbb{C}}^{g}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and we call the degeneration <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">maximal</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of a maximal
degeneration, the intermediate
cover <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of the punctured family admits an analytic open embedding <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{*}\hookrightarrow({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}\times{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}\hookrightarrow({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}\times{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
into an algebraic torus, with the map
to <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the projection
to the second factor.
Thus, by the theory of toric varieties, it is possible
to extend <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{*}\to\Delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}\to\Delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
to a family <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\to\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
More precisely, we take a toric
extension of <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}\times{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}\times{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
for which the fiber-preserving action of <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm gr}_{0}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm gr}_{0}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> extends to an action
on the central fiber <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The quotient of <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\to\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>
by the extended action of <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm gr}_{0}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm gr}_{0}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
produces a new model of the degeneration
with particularly nice properties,
e.g. toroidal singularities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The original paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> performs
this construction more generally over any
complete ring, corresponding to a possibly
higher-dimensional base,
and using formal algebraic geometry.
Mumford’s work was inspired by an influential
1959 manuscript of Tate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>
on degenerating elliptic curves.
In the late 1970’s,
Nakamura and Namikawa
worked out the theory in the complex-analytic
setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>,
culminating in a method of patching together
Mumford constructions over the cones of the second
Voronoi fan,
to produce a relatively proper, analytic
extension of the universal abelian variety
<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to a toroidal
compactification of <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">An impressive, and notoriously technical, further
advancement was the work of Faltings–Chai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>
in the early 1990’s, who extended the Mumford construction
and the theory of toroidal compactifications
to the arithmetic setting. In the later
1990’s, Alexeev–Nakamura <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>
and Alexeev <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>
used the Mumford construction to compactify the universal
abelian torsor <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{X}}_{g}^{\star},\Theta_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{X}}_{g}^{\star},\Theta_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> with theta
divisor, cf. Construction <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Construction 6.6. ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>,
and provide a modular interpretation
of this compactification, as the normalization
of the closure of the space of
KSBA-stable pairs <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\epsilon\Theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\epsilon\Theta)</annotation></semantics></math>
in the proper DM stack of log
general type varieties.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Contents</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of this paper is
to provide a “working mathematician’s guide”
to the Mumford construction.
Thus, we usually work analytically over <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
though we do also touch on the question of algebraicity
of Mumford constructions (Prop. <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6.2. Algebraicity and projectivity ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>).
Furthermore, we largely restrict our attention
to maximal degenerations, though most of the
results presented here apply
in the non-maximal case.
Many of the ideas of the paper are to be
found scattered through the literature; some are
difficult to find, and others appear to be new,
such as Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ Regular matroids ‣ 1.1. Contents ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After reviewing in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
preliminary material on principally
polarized abelian varieties, their Hodge theory,
their moduli, their degenerations,
and toroidal extensions and compactifications of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we dive into the main constructions in Section
<a href="#S3" title="3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Mumford constructions and examples</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using tools from toric geometry, we construct
maximal degenerations of principally polarized
abelian <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-folds.
The constructions are presented in increasing
levels of generality and are broadly divided
into two classes: <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fan</span> constructions
and <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">polytope</span> constructions (see Section
<a href="#S1.SS2" title="1.2. Index of constructions ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> for a list). The fan construction
only produces a degeneration complex-analytically,
but has the advantage of being relatively simpler,
and having more readable geometry.
The advantage of
the polytope construction is that it
outputs a relatively projective degeneration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The equality
of the two constructions is examined in Section
<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Comparison of polytope and fan constructions ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, while the
topology is discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Weight filtration and dual complex ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
where we analyze the weight filtration
on the limiting mixed Hodge structure of a general fiber.
We also study the effect, on Mumford constructions,
of a toroidal base change and
of replacing the polarization by a multiple,
see Section <a href="#S3.SS6" title="3.6. Base change and Veronese embedding ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Regular matroids</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We continue in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> with
a more detailed analysis of Mumford constructions
associated to regular matroids
(Constr. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem16" title="Construction 4.16 (Shifted matroidal degenerations). ‣ 4.3. Shifted and transversely shifted matroidal degenerations ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.16</span></a>). A
<span id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">matroid</span> <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a collection of subsets of a finite set <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>,
encoding the notion
of linear
independence of a set
of vectors in a vector space
(Def. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 4.1. ‣ 4.1. Matroids, graphs, and quadratic forms ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).
An embedding <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\hookrightarrow{\mathbb{F}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\hookrightarrow{\mathbb{F}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
into a vector space
over a field <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, realizing this
collection of independent sets,
is a <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">realization</span> of <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Matroids
which admit a realization over any field
are <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">regular</span> (Def. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 4.2. ‣ 4.1. Matroids, graphs, and quadratic forms ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>),
though <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> suffice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Associated to a regular matroid <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are
the so-called <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">matroidal</span>, <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">shifted matroidal</span>,
and <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">transversely shifted matroidal</span>
Mumford constructions, see Sections <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Mumford degenerations associated to regular matroids ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Shifted and transversely shifted matroidal degenerations ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.
Related degenerations
were explored for cographic
matroids by Dancso–McBreen–Shende
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Sec. 8.3]</cite>, building on
unpublished work of Hausel–Proudfoot.
Perhaps unsurprisingly, regular matroids
are intimately connected to the
total space of a Mumford
construction being regular, i.e. smooth. In fact,
a Mumford construction <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
(i) <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is regular, and (ii) over each coordinate
hyperplane <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{u_{i}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{i}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, of the polydisk,
the vanishing cycles span a <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space,
is necessarily a transversely shifted
matroidal degeneration, and vice versa
(Props. <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 5.3. ‣ 5.1. Mumford degenerations with nodal singularities ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 5.13. ‣ 5.2. Weight filtration of a semistable morphism ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As we explain in Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, for a
family <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional PPAVs, the monodromy about the
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate hyperplane defines,
via the principal polarization, a symmetric matrix
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in{\rm{Sym}}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in{\rm{Sym}}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
(Def. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Definition 2.6. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>). The cone in
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Sym}}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Sym}}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{1},\dotsc,B_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{1},\dotsc,B_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the <em id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">monodromy cone</em>
of the family <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> (Def. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Definition 2.7. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>).
Such a cone is <em id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">matroidal</em> if it
is induced by an integral realization of a
regular matroid (Def. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Definition 4.6. ‣ 4.1. Matroids, graphs, and quadratic forms ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>).
Transversely shifted matroidal degenerations
are examples; they are of particular importance to our
companion paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth family
of PPAVs of dimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, whose monodromy cone
is matroidal (Defs. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Definition 2.7. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Definition 4.6. ‣ 4.1. Matroids, graphs, and quadratic forms ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>).
There is a flat, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-trivial extension
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a
</span>nodal<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> morphism (Def. <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 5.1. ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>), and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> may be assumed </span>strictly nodal<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
if the monodromy about each coordinate hyperplane
is imprimitive.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, given <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> generators
of a matroidal cone, there exists a family
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\ast}\colon X^{\ast}\to(\Delta^{\ast})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\ast}\colon X^{\ast}\to(\Delta^{\ast})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
of PPAVs whose monodromies are the
specified generators, and an extension as above,
which is
the restriction
of a projective family over a quasiprojective variety
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> to a polydisk <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">See Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> and Corollary
<a href="#S7.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 7.2. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>, respectively, for
more algebraic formulations of the first and
second statements of the above theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As a particular application, the
relative intermediate Jacobian
fibration <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IJY^{\circ}\to(\Delta^{*})^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IJY^{\circ}\to(\Delta^{*})^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the punctured universal deformation <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\circ}\to(\Delta^{*})^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\circ}\to(\Delta^{*})^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the Segre cubic <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (Ex. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem16" title="Example 2.16 (Degenerations of intermediate Jacobians). ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a>)
admits such a filling <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IJY\to\Delta^{10}\simeq{\rm Def}_{Y_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>Def</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IJY\to\Delta^{10}\simeq{\rm Def}_{Y_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>
as does the relative Jacobian fibration
of the universal deformation of a nodal
curve
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (Exs. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem15" title="Example 2.15 (Degenerations of Jacobians). ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.15</span></a>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem13" title="Example 4.13 (Cographic matroids). ‣ 4.2. Mumford degenerations associated to regular matroids ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>).
The respective matroids are
the Seymour–Bixby matroid <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{R}_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{R}_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>
and the cographic matroid <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{*}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{*}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>
of the dual graph <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\Gamma(C_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\Gamma(C_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
(Exs. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Example 4.7 (Seymour-Bixby []). ‣ 4.1. Matroids, graphs, and quadratic forms ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Example 4.3. ‣ 4.1. Matroids, graphs, and quadratic forms ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Nodal and semistable morphisms</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our investigations connect in a very natural way
to the notion of a <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semistable morphism</span>
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>, introduced by Abramovich–Karu
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and the more restrictive
notion of a <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">nodal morphism</span>
(Def. <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 5.1. ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>). The former are morphisms
between smooth spaces <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, which
are étale or analytically-locally a
product of snc degenerations <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x^{(1)}\cdots x^{(m)}=u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(1)}\cdots x^{(m)}=u</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the latter (nodal morphisms) additionally
satisfy <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The main result of Adiprasito–Liu–Temkin
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> is that, for <span id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span>
dominant morphism <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
there is
a regular
alteration <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> of the base and a birational
modification of the base change <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to X\times_{Y}Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to X\times_{Y}Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}\colon X^{\prime}\to Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}\colon X^{\prime}\to Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is semistable.
Furthermore, once semistability is achieved, further
base changes admit a functorial semistable resolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Once we have proven that transversely
shifted matroidal
degenerations are nodal morphisms, we continue
with a general analysis of the Hodge theory
of semistable morphisms, in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
We define a multi-parameter analogue
(Prop. <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Proposition 5.10. ‣ 5.2. Weight filtration of a semistable morphism ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>) of the
Clemens retraction for <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter semistable
degenerations, and investigate the relationship
(Prop. <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem11" title="Proposition 5.11. ‣ 5.2. Weight filtration of a semistable morphism ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>)
between the dual complex of the central
fiber, and the graded piece
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the weight filtration
on <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Resolution of the base change of a nodal morphism ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, we
instantiate
explicitly
the functorial resolution of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Thm. 4.4]</cite>,
in the case of a base change of a nodal morphism
(Thm. <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem14" title="Theorem 5.14. ‣ 5.3. Resolution of the base change of a nodal morphism ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a>).
We apply this resolution algorithm to
transversely shifted matroidal
degenerations in Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. Resolution of the base change of a transversely shifted matroidal degeneration ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.
Refinements of these results will play an
important role in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Sec. 3 and 4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">The second Voronoi
fan and Alexeev’s theorem</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we review the work of Alexeev, Nakamura,
Namikawa and Faltings–Chai
on the extension of the universal family
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> over the toroidal compactification
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\rm vor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>vor</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\rm vor}</annotation></semantics></math> associated
to a distinguished fan
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}_{\rm vor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔉</mi><mi>vor</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}_{\rm vor}</annotation></semantics></math> (Defs.
<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 6.1. ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>, <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 6.4. ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>),
whose support is the cone <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>
of positive semi-definite <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\times g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\times g</annotation></semantics></math>
matrices with rational null space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We sketch a proof of Alexeev’s theorem that
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\rm vor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>vor</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\rm vor}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the normalization of the
KSBA compactification of the moduli space of
abelian torsors with theta divisor <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\epsilon\Theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\epsilon\Theta)</annotation></semantics></math>,
paying particular attention to the subtle differences
between Alexeev’s universal family
<math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}^{\star}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}^{\star}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and the universal family of
abelian varieties <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
(Constr. <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Construction 6.6. ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> and Rem. <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem8" title="Remark 6.8. ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also provide a brief review of the extensive
literature on the cones of the
second Voronoi fan, for <math id="S1.SS1.SSSx4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>
(Ex. <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem7" title="Example 6.7 (Second Voronoi fan for 𝑔≤6). ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Index of constructions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The various forms of the Mumford construction presented
in this paper are thus:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Construction 3.3 (1-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>):</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Via fans,
over a <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter base (i.e., a disk),
and over a family of <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter bases,
complete with respect to a fixed monodromy operator <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\colon H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})\to H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\colon H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})\to H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
encoded by a symmetric bilinear form
<math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>):</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Via fans, over a polydisk <math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
over a family of such polydisks,
complete with respect to a fixed collection
of monodromy bilinear forms <math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>):</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Via fans,
over the toroidal extension
<math id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
associated to a rational
polyhedral cone <math id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>):</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Via polytopes,
over a polydisk <math id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
associated to a collection <math id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b_{1},\dots,b_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b_{1},\dots,b_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of convex piecewise linear
functions <math id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{g}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{g}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> with appropriate <math id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>-periodicity, and over a family of polydisks,
complete with respect to the associated
monodromy cone <math id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem26" title="Construction 3.26. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.26</span></a>):</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Via polytopes,
over the toroidal extension
<math id="S1.I1.ix5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
associated to a rational
polyhedral cone <math id="S1.I1.ix5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem38" title="Construction 3.38. ‣ 3.6. Base change and Veronese embedding ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.38</span></a>):</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Via polytopes,
as in (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>),
but where only <math id="S1.I1.ix6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> times the principal polarization extends
to a relatively ample line bundle on the family.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem16" title="Construction 4.16 (Shifted matroidal degenerations). ‣ 4.3. Shifted and transversely shifted matroidal degenerations ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.16</span></a>):</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As special cases of
(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem38" title="Construction 3.38. ‣ 3.6. Base change and Veronese embedding ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.38</span></a>),
associated to a regular matroid <math id="S1.I1.ix7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
of rank <math id="S1.I1.ix7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and a hyperplane arrangement inducing
this regular matroid from the set of normal vectors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Construction 6.5 (Mumford construction of second Voronoi type). ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>):</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Associated
to a “tautological” version of (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem26" title="Construction 3.26. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.26</span></a>)
for a cone <math id="S1.I1.ix8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\in{\mathfrak{F}}_{\rm vor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔉</mi><mi>vor</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\in{\mathfrak{F}}_{\rm vor}</annotation></semantics></math> of the second Voronoi fan,
giving a local
analytic extension of the universal family
of abelian varieties.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem6" title="Construction 6.6. ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>):</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As in (<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Construction 6.5 (Mumford construction of second Voronoi type). ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>), but
giving an extension <math id="S1.I1.ix9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{X}}^{\star}_{g}}^{\rm vor}\to\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\rm vor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>vor</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>vor</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{X}}^{\star}_{g}}^{\rm vor}\to\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\rm vor}</annotation></semantics></math> of the universal family
of abelian torsors with theta divisor.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An extensive collection of examples (Exs. <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem31" title="Example 3.31. ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.31</span></a>,
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem34" title="Example 3.34 (Theta graph, 1-parameter). ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.34</span></a>, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem35" title="Example 3.35 (Theta graph, 3-parameter). ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.35</span></a>, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem40" title="Example 3.40 (Base change and resolution of the Tate curve). ‣ 3.6. Base change and Veronese embedding ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.40</span></a>,
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem13" title="Example 4.13 (Cographic matroids). ‣ 4.2. Mumford degenerations associated to regular matroids ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem14" title="Example 4.14 (Matroidal Mumford degeneration on 𝑅̱_10). ‣ 4.2. Mumford degenerations associated to regular matroids ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem20" title="Example 4.20 (Transversely shifted matroidal degeneration for the theta graph). ‣ 4.3. Shifted and transversely shifted matroidal degenerations ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.20</span></a>,
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem21" title="Example 4.21. ‣ 4.3. Shifted and transversely shifted matroidal degenerations ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.21</span></a>, <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Example 5.16. ‣ 5.4. Resolution of the base change of a transversely shifted matroidal degeneration ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a>, <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem7" title="Example 6.7 (Second Voronoi fan for 𝑔≤6). ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>),
with figures, is also
provided in the text,
see especially Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
The first of these
(Ex. <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem31" title="Example 3.31. ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.31</span></a>) is the prerequisite
ur-example of the Mumford construction:
the <span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tate curve</span>, i.e. the extension
of the family <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}/u^{\mathbb{Z}}\to\Delta_{u}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}/u^{\mathbb{Z}}\to\Delta_{u}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
of elliptic curves by an irreducible nodal curve.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Acknowledgments</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thank Valery Alexeev, Younghan Bae, David Holmes,
and Mirko Mauri for useful discussions and comments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">PE was partially supported by NSF grant DMS-2401104.
OdGF and StS have received funding from the European Research Council (ERC) under the
European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under grant agreement
N<sup id="S1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S1.SS3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">o</span></sup> 948066 (ERC-StG RationAlgic).
OdGF has also received funding from the ERC Consolidator Grant FourSurf N<sup id="S1.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S1.SS3.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">o</span></sup> 101087365.
The research was partly conducted in the framework of the DFG-funded research training group RTG 2965: From Geometry to Numbers.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminary material</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Algebraic and analytic stacks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By a
<em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DM algebraic stack</em>, or simply <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DM stack</em>,
we will mean a separated Deligne–Mumford stack of finite
type over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, a <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DM analytic stack</em> will
be a separated Deligne–Mumford analytic stack <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in the
sense of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, Def. 5.2]</cite>. Thus, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a stack on
the site of complex analytic spaces such that the diagonal
is representable and finite and there exists an analytic
space <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and a surjective étale morphism <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is locally modeled as a finite
quotient of an analytic space, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, Prop. 5.4]</cite>,
and that the analytification of a DM algebraic stack is
a DM analytic stack, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, Lem. 5.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Principally polarized abelian varieties</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the DM stack
of principally polarized abelian varieties (PPAVs)
of dimension <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, over <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since a PPAV <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is uniquely determined by
its polarized weight <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>
Hodge structure on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
the period map defines an isomorphism
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\simeq\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\simeq\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to
an arithmetic quotient of a
Hermitian symmetric domain of Type III.
We review this construction now.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-polarized
Hodge structure <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\bullet,\bullet},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\bullet,\bullet},L)</annotation></semantics></math>
of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span>
is a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> together
with an integral, non-degenerate,
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-symmetric bilinear form
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\colon V_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes V_{\mathbb{Z}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\colon V_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes V_{\mathbb{Z}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and a Hodge decomposition</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}\coloneqq V_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}=\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p,q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}\coloneqq V_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}=\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying the following conditions:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{q,p}=\overline{H^{p,q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{q,p}=\overline{H^{p,q}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(H^{p,q},H^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(H^{p,q},H^{p^{\prime},q^{\prime}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{k(k-1)/2}i^{p-q}L(\bar{v},v)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{k(k-1)/2}i^{p-q}L(\bar{v},v)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\neq v\in H^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≠</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\neq v\in H^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-polarized
Hodge structure <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\bullet,\bullet},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{\mathbb{Z}},H^{\bullet,\bullet},L)</annotation></semantics></math>
is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">principally polarized</span>
if the pairing <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is unimodular.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)</annotation></semantics></math> be a unimodular
symplectic lattice, and
consider the Lagrangian Grassmannian
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm LGr}(V_{\mathbb{C}},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>LGr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm LGr}(V_{\mathbb{C}},L)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is the projective flag variety of isotropic <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional
subspaces of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The polarized weight <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>
Hodge structures on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)</annotation></semantics></math> with a Hodge decomposition
of the form <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{-1,0}\oplus H^{0,-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{C}}=H^{-1,0}\oplus H^{0,-1}</annotation></semantics></math> define
an analytic open subset of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm LGr}(V_{\mathbb{C}},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>LGr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm LGr}(V_{\mathbb{C}},L)</annotation></semantics></math>, given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{H^{-1,0}\subset V_{\mathbb{C}}\,:\,L|_{H^{-1,0}}=0\textrm{ and }iL(\bar{v},v)&gt;0\textrm{ for }0\neq v\in H^{-1,0}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{H^{-1,0}\subset V_{\mathbb{C}}\,:\,L|_{H^{-1,0}}=0\textrm{ and }iL(\bar{v},v)&gt;0\textrm{ for }0\neq v\in H^{-1,0}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[H^{-1,0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[H^{-1,0}]</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.2. Principally polarized abelian varieties ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>),
we may define a complex torus</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\coloneqq V_{\mathbb{C}}/(V_{\mathbb{Z}}+H^{-1,0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\coloneqq V_{\mathbb{C}}/(V_{\mathbb{Z}}+H^{-1,0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We have canonical
isomorphisms <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq V_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq V_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the symplectic form
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in V_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\vee}\wedge V_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in V_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\vee}\wedge V_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> defines an element</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L\in\wedge^{2}H^{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq H^{2}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0em">∈</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\wedge^{2}H^{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq H^{2}(X,{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The condition that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{-1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{-1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> is Lagrangian for <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>
amounts to the property that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in H^{1,1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in H^{1,1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is a
Hodge class. Hence
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> determines
a holomorphic line bundle <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\to X</annotation></semantics></math>,
unique up to translation by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Pic}^{0}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Pic}^{0}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, the
condition <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="iL(\bar{v},v)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">iL(\bar{v},v)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that any
lift <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is ample, and so
in fact, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an abelian variety (i.e. projective).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Choosing a standard symplectic basis of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
produces an isometry <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}^{2g},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}^{2g},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{2g}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by vectors
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i},f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i},f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,g</annotation></semantics></math> and the unimodular
symplectic form <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfies <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\cdot e_{j}=f_{i}\cdot f_{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\cdot e_{j}=f_{i}\cdot f_{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\cdot f_{j}=\delta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\cdot f_{j}=\delta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Siegel upper half-space</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the space of symmetric <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\times g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\times g</annotation></semantics></math> matrices
with positive-definite
imaginary part.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A choice of symplectic basis of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)</annotation></semantics></math> identifies
(<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.2. Principally polarized abelian varieties ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In a standard symplectic basis, the Lagrangian
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{-1,0}\subset V_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{-1,0}\subset V_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the span of the columns of some
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g\times g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g\times g</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">period matrix</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}\sigma\\
I\end{pmatrix}\in{\rm Mat}_{2g\times g}({\mathbb{C}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>σ</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Mat</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}\sigma\\
I\end{pmatrix}\in{\rm Mat}_{2g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which we write in <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> block form.
The condition that <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in H^{1,1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in H^{1,1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is
Hodge amounts to the symmetry
of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, while the positivity condition
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="iL(\bar{v},v)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">iL(\bar{v},v)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> amounts
to the fact that the imaginary part
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Im}(\sigma)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Im}(\sigma)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is positive-definite.
Hence <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Changes of symplectic basis,
i.e. elements of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
act on the left, by
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> block matrices,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B\\
C&amp;D\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\sigma\\
I\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}A\sigma+B\\
C\sigma+D\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>σ</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B\\
C&amp;D\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\sigma\\
I\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}A\sigma+B\\
C\sigma+D\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Renormalizing the generators of our Lagrangian subspace
to be dual to the <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the symplectic form
corresponds to right multiplication by <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C\sigma+D)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C\sigma+D)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So we get the Lagrangian corresponding to
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\sigma+B)(C\sigma+D)^{-1}\in{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\sigma+B)(C\sigma+D)^{-1}\in{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is
the standard action of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Sp}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Sp}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The pair <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,L)</annotation></semantics></math> is called a
<span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">principally polarized
abelian variety</span>,
or PPAV.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For any representative <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\in{\rm Pic}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\in{\rm Pic}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(X,{\mathcal{L}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(X,{\mathcal{L}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and so there is a unique divisor
<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta\in|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta\in|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math> called the <span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">theta divisor</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It follows
from the above discussion that
the moduli stack of PPAVs <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,L)</annotation></semantics></math>
is given by the orbifold
(i.e. smooth DM
analytic stack)
<math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\simeq\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\simeq\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, the universal family <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
of PPAVs is uniformized by <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{g}\times{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{g}\times{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and can
be presented as a quotient, too:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\simeq\left({\mathbb{Z}}^{2g}\rtimes\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\right)\backslash\left({\mathbb{C}}^{g}\times{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\simeq\left({\mathbb{Z}}^{2g}\rtimes\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\right)\backslash\left({\mathbb{C}}^{g}\times{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Degenerations of PPAVs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following sections,
we discuss the monodromy of
degenerations of PPAVs, especially
in relation to toroidal extensions
of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>
for reference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon(X,L)\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon(X,L)\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a degeneration of
PPAVs of dimension <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> over a polydisk <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with
coordinates <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\dotsc,u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\dotsc,u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the discriminant
locus is the union of the coordinate hyperplanes
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(u_{i})=\{u_{i}=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{i})=\{u_{i}=0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a
base point <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and
let <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the monodromy transformation
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}\colon V_{\mathbb{Z}}\to V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}\colon V_{\mathbb{Z}}\to V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to
the simple, oriented loop
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}\in\pi_{1}((\Delta^{*})^{k},t)\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}\in\pi_{1}((\Delta^{*})^{k},t)\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is unipotent—for instance, by a result of Clemens
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Thm. 7.36]</cite>, this holds if the general fiber
over <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> has reduced normal crossings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Choosing a symplectic basis <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}^{2g},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L)\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}^{2g},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>,
we may view <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as acting on the reference lattice <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{2g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=\log(T_{i})=T_{i}-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=\log(T_{i})=T_{i}-I</annotation></semantics></math> be its logarithm,
where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the identity matrix of size
<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g\times 2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g\times 2g</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{2}_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{2}_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}\circ N_{j}=N_{j}\circ N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}\circ N_{j}=N_{j}\circ N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> commute. Let
<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
be any strictly positive linear combination.
Then <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the monodromy
of the restriction of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to the cocharacter
<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by
<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\mapsto(u^{r_{1}},\dots,u^{r_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\mapsto(u^{r_{1}},\dots,u^{r_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Thm. 3.3]</cite>,
all <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{1},\dots,r_{k})\in{\mathbb{N}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{1},\dots,r_{k})\in{\mathbb{N}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
define the same increasing
<span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weight filtration</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{-2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq({\rm im}\,N)^{\rm sat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>sat</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq({\rm im}\,N)^{\rm sat}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq\ker N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq\ker N</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq V_{\mathbb{Z}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq V_{\mathbb{Z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">More generally, for any
<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{1},\dots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{1},\dots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the filtration so defined depends only on the polyhedral
face of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The above weight filtration may also be
described as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle W_{-2}=\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}{\rm im}\,N_{i}\right)^{\rm sat}\quad\text{and}\quad W_{-1}=\bigcap_{i=1}^{k}\ker(N_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>sat</mi></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle W_{-2}=\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}{\rm im}\,N_{i}\right)^{\rm sat}\quad\text{and}\quad W_{-1}=\bigcap_{i=1}^{k}\ker(N_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Indeed, by the saturatedness of the above
filtration, it suffices to prove this for
rational, and, in fact, for real coefficients.
The inclusions <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm im}(\sum N_{i})\subset\sum{\rm im}\,N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm im}(\sum N_{i})\subset\sum{\rm im}\,N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcap\ker(N_{i})\subset\ker(\sum N_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcap\ker(N_{i})\subset\ker(\sum N_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
are clear.
To prove the converse, let <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{1},\dots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{1},\dots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and note that
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm im}(\sum r_{i}N_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm im}(\sum r_{i}N_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker(\sum r_{i}N_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker(\sum r_{i}N_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
do not depend on the choice of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Thm. 3.3]</cite>.
The inclusions in question thus follow from a limit
argument where <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>, applied to <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm im}(\sum r_{i}N_{i})\subset{\rm im}(\sum N_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm im}(\sum r_{i}N_{i})\subset{\rm im}(\sum N_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker(\sum N_{i})\subset\ker(\sum r_{i}N_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker(\sum N_{i})\subset\ker(\sum r_{i}N_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the standard Lagrangian subspace
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}e_{1}\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}e_{g}\subset({\mathbb{Z}}^{2g},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}e_{1}\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}e_{g}\subset({\mathbb{Z}}^{2g},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Its stabilizer is
the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parabolic group</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq\left\{\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B\\
0&amp;A^{-T}\end{pmatrix}\in\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq\left\{\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B\\
0&amp;A^{-T}\end{pmatrix}\in\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BA^{T}=AB^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BA^{T}=AB^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define
the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unipotent subgroup</span> of
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq\left\{\begin{pmatrix}I&amp;B\\
0&amp;I\end{pmatrix}\colon B\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.278em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq\left\{\begin{pmatrix}I&amp;B\\
0&amp;I\end{pmatrix}\colon B\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Levi quotient</span> to be
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}/U_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}/U_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which can also
be lifted into <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Sp}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Sp}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> as the block
diagonal matrices (i.e. matrices with
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The collection of commuting unipotent matrices
<math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}\in{\rm Sp}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}\in{\rm Sp}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
can be simultaneously conjugated into
the unipotent subgroup <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
as they fix a coisotropic space
(given by <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>) and hence fix a Lagrangian
subspace.
Thus, choosing a basis appropriately,
we may assume that the monodromies
<math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are all of the form</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{i}=\begin{pmatrix}I&amp;B_{i}\\
0&amp;I\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{i}=\begin{pmatrix}I&amp;B_{i}\\
0&amp;I\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">for symmetric matrices <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon(X,L)\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon(X,L)\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a degeneration
of PPAVs with unipotent monodromies
about the coordinate
hyperplanes. We define the
<span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monodromy bilinear
forms</span>
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math> by the formula</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B_{i}(x,y)=L(N_{i}x,y)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B_{i}(x,y)=L(N_{i}x,y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Observe that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> depends only
on the punctured family
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\colon(X^{*},L^{*})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\colon(X^{*},L^{*})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
and so the definition
extends naturally to families of PPAVs
over the punctured polydisk.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This provides a coordinate-free definition of
the matrices <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
from above.
Implicit in the above definition is the claim that <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
contains <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> in its kernel, which
follows from (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a symplectic basis of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has the form (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)
for each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, which identifies
each
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with a symmetric matrix
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The span</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}\coloneqq{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\,\dots,\,B_{k}\}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}\coloneqq{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\,\dots,\,B_{k}\}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monodromy cone</span>
associated to the degeneration
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon(X,L)\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon(X,L)\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This definition extends to any
family of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional PPAVs
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\ast}\colon(X^{\ast},L^{\ast})\to(\Delta^{\ast})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\ast}\colon(X^{\ast},L^{\ast})\to(\Delta^{\ast})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
with unipotent monodromy.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the collection <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{i})_{i=1,\dots,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{i})_{i=1,\dots,k}</annotation></semantics></math>
of monodromy matrices, and hence the
monodromy cone <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, is unique,
up to the simultaneous action of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
by <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\mapsto AB_{i}A^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\mapsto AB_{i}A^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. This action
corresponds to the conjugation action
<math id="S2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}\mapsto MT_{i}M^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}\mapsto MT_{i}M^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset{\rm{Sp}}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset{\rm{Sp}}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in P_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in P_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which descends
to the Levi quotient
<math id="S2.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}/U_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}/U_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> because
<math id="S2.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The symplectic basis of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
determines a lift of the classifying map
<math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to a
holomorphic period map <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}\colon{\mathbb{H}}^{k}\to{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}\colon{\mathbb{H}}^{k}\to{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{k}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{k}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the universal cover,
<math id="S2.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}\coloneqq\left\{\tau\in{\mathbb{C}}\mid\im(\tau)&gt;0\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}\coloneqq\left\{\tau\in{\mathbb{C}}\mid\im(\tau)&gt;0\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Take coordinates
<math id="S2.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{1},\dots,\tau_{k})\in{\mathbb{H}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{1},\dots,\tau_{k})\in{\mathbb{H}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the universal
covering map given by <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}={\rm exp}(2\pi i\tau_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}={\rm exp}(2\pi i\tau_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This lifted map satisfies the equivariance property</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau_{1},\dots,\tau_{i}+1,\dots,\tau_{k})=T_{i}\cdot\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau_{1},\dots,\tau_{k})=\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau_{1},\dots,\tau_{k})+B_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau_{1},\dots,\tau_{i}+1,\dots,\tau_{k})=T_{i}\cdot\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau_{1},\dots,\tau_{k})=\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau_{1},\dots,\tau_{k})+B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">corresponding to the deck transformation for the
generator <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}\in\pi_{1}((\Delta^{*})^{k},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}\in\pi_{1}((\Delta^{*})^{k},t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define a holomorphic map to the flag variety
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}\coloneqq{\rm LGr}(V_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>LGr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}\coloneqq{\rm LGr}(V_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}},L)</annotation></semantics></math>
by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{\Psi}\colon{\mathbb{H}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{\Psi}\colon{\mathbb{H}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\to{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\to{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tau</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau)-(\tau_{1}B_{1}+\dots+\tau_{k}B_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau)-(\tau_{1}B_{1}+\dots+\tau_{k}B_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and denote by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>
its descent to <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math> descends
because the <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-(\tau_{1}B_{1}+\cdots+\tau_{k}B_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-(\tau_{1}B_{1}+\cdots+\tau_{k}B_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>
term cancels the equivariance
of <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so makes the map invariant
under the action of <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now recall Schmid’s multivariable nilpotent
orbit theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, Thm. 4.12]</cite>,
applied to our setting:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
extends to a holomorphic map
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}\to{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}\to{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(0)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the extension to the origin,
and consider <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau)\coloneqq\Psi(0)+(\tau_{1}B_{1}+\dots+\tau_{k}B_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>nilp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau)\coloneqq\Psi(0)+(\tau_{1}B_{1}+\dots+\tau_{k}B_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau)\in{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\subset{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>nilp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau)\in{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\subset{\mathbb{D}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for
all sufficiently large </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Im}\,\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Im}\,\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the distance
</span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau),\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>nilp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau),\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
decays exponentially in </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Im}\,\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Im}\,\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Here distance is measured in the natural left
<math id="S2.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant metric on <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}\coloneqq\{B\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})\,:\,B&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}\coloneqq\{B\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})\,:\,B&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the cone of positive-definite matrices
and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be its <span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rational closure</span>,
consisting of all positive semi-definite
matrices whose kernel is a rational subspace
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">There is a natural stratification</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}=\textstyle{\mathcal{P}}_{g}\sqcup\bigsqcup_{V_{1}}{\mathcal{P}}_{g-1}\sqcup\bigsqcup_{V_{2}}{\mathcal{P}}_{g-2}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup\bigsqcup_{V_{g}}{\mathcal{P}}_{0}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>⨆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mstyle><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>⨆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mstyle><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>⨆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}=\textstyle{\mathcal{P}}_{g}\sqcup\bigsqcup_{V_{1}}{\mathcal{P}}_{g-1}\sqcup\bigsqcup_{V_{2}}{\mathcal{P}}_{g-2}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup\bigsqcup_{V_{g}}{\mathcal{P}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}\subset{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}\subset{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> range
over all primitive integral
sublattices of <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
of dimension <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and the
relevant copy of <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g-i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cone of positive-definite
bilinear forms on <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{g}/(V_{i}\otimes{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{g}/(V_{i}\otimes{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. See Figure
<a href="#S6.F22" title="Figure 22 ‣ Example 6.7 (Second Voronoi fan for 𝑔≤6). ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> to visualize
the projectivization of <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{2}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{2}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a cusped
hyperbolic disk.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from item (1) of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 2.9. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> that:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The monodromy cone <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>
is contained in the rational closure <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> of
the positive-definite
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\times g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\times g</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, uniquely
up to the action of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We have that <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-2}^{\perp}=W_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-2}^{\perp}=W_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> where
the perpendicular is taken with respect to <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> descends to a unimodular
symplectic form on <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}/W_{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}/W_{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, Thm. 6.16]</cite>, we have the following fundamental
theorem on the existence of the limit MHS:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tuple <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L,\Psi(0),W_{\bullet})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{\mathbb{Z}},L,\Psi(0),W_{\bullet})</annotation></semantics></math> defines a
graded-polarized <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-mixed Hodge structure,
the </span>limit mixed Hodge structure<span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
In particular, the filtration of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}/W_{-2}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}/W_{-2}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
induced by the Lagrangian subspace
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(0)</annotation></semantics></math> defines a pure, principally
polarized Hodge structure of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-1}/W_{-2}\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}^{2h},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-1}/W_{-2}\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}^{2h},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is the rank of the null space
of a general element
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">maximally degenerate</span>
if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}\cap{\mathcal{P}}_{g}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}\cap{\mathcal{P}}_{g}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the general element
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive-definite.
Equivalently,
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-2}=W_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-2}=W_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the
<span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tropical moduli space of abelian varieties</span> to be</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}\coloneqq\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>trop</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}\coloneqq\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the action is via <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\mapsto ABA^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\mapsto ABA^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We can view <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>trop</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}</annotation></semantics></math> as the tropical
moduli space of abelian varieties, because, as we will see
in Section <a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, a fan defining
a toroidal extension of <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> lives naturally in
<math id="S2.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>trop</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}</annotation></semantics></math>. But more deeply, <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>trop</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}</annotation></semantics></math>
(or at least, the image of <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> in it)
is itself a moduli space of “tropical abelian varieties”
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>:
It parametrizes isometry classes of full rank
lattices in <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the action of <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
serves the role of forgetting the basis of the lattice
in which the Gram matrix of the corresponding real
intersection form has been expanded.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> defines, canonically, an immersed
polyhedral cone in <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>trop</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.15</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Degenerations of Jacobians)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon C\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon C\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of nodal curves,
which is smooth over the complement of the coordinate hyperplanes
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(u_{1}\cdots u_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{1}\cdots u_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{ij}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{ij}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the nodes of the
general fiber over <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The local equation of the smoothing
of the node <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is given
by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{ij}y_{ij}=u_{i}^{r_{ij}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{ij}y_{ij}=u_{i}^{r_{ij}}</annotation></semantics></math> for
some positive integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows from
the Picard-Lefschetz formula that
the logarithm of monodromy
on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{1}(C_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{1}(C_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> about the
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate hyperplane is
given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle N_{i}\colon x\mapsto-\sum_{j}r_{ij}(x\cdot\gamma_{ij})\gamma_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle N_{i}\colon x\mapsto-\sum_{j}r_{ij}(x\cdot\gamma_{ij})\gamma_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{ij}\in H_{1}(C_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{ij}\in H_{1}(C_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> is
(either orientation of)
the vanishing cycle of the node <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
is the intersection form on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Observe that the total space <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
is smooth if and only if all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{ij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{ij}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and no
node of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the specialization of a node over
both <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(u_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Taking the relative Jacobian fibration</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\pi^{\circ}\colon JC^{\circ}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\pi^{\circ}\colon JC^{\circ}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.3" class="ltx_p">of the smooth family,
the monodromy is given by the same formula,
since <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(JC_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq H_{1}(C_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(JC_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq H_{1}(C_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The weight filtration is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-2}={\mathbb{Z}}\textrm{-span}\{\gamma_{ij}\}\textrm{ and }W_{-1}=(W_{-2})^{\perp}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-span</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-2}={\mathbb{Z}}\textrm{-span}\{\gamma_{ij}\}\textrm{ and }W_{-1}=(W_{-2})^{\perp}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Computing the monodromy bilinear forms
on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> we get</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle B_{i}(x,y)\coloneqq N_{i}(x)\cdot y=\sum_{j}r_{ij}(x\cdot\gamma_{ij})(y\cdot\gamma_{ij})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle B_{i}(x,y)\coloneqq N_{i}(x)\cdot y=\sum_{j}r_{ij}(x\cdot\gamma_{ij})(y\cdot\gamma_{ij}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Suppose now that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon C\to\Delta^{k}\subset{\rm Def}(C_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Def</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon C\to\Delta^{k}\subset{\rm Def}(C_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
is a slice of the universal deformation of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
which is transversal to the
equisingular/locally trivial deformations.
Then there is only one node over each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding
integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of nodes of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}(x,x)=(\gamma_{i}\cdot x)^{2}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}})^{\vee}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}(x,x)=(\gamma_{i}\cdot x)^{2}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.6" class="ltx_p">is a rank <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> quadratic form,
given by the square of the linear form which is
pairing with the vanishing cycle
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}\in{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}\in{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We have canonical isomorphisms</p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\simeq H_{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\simeq H_{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\simeq H^{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\simeq H^{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(C_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(C_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
is the dual graph of the central fiber;
indeed, by duality it suffices to prove the first
isomorphism, which follows e.g. from
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Proposition 5.12. ‣ 5.2. Weight filtration of a semistable morphism ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a> below.
The space
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}={\rm gr}_{-2}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}={\rm gr}_{-2}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated
by the vanishing cycles
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which are in bijection with the edges of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(C_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(C_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The relations between the vanishing cycles <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are given
by the boundaries of the subsurfaces they bound. In terms of graphs,
these are the coboundaries of the vertices of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(C_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(C_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm gr}_{-2}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq{\rm coker}({\mathcal{C}}^{0}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})\xrightarrow{\partial}{\mathcal{C}}^{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}}))\eqqcolon H^{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∂</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm gr}_{-2}^{W}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq{\rm coker}({\mathcal{C}}^{0}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})\xrightarrow{\partial}{\mathcal{C}}^{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}}))\eqqcolon H^{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.3" class="ltx_p">In turn,
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
with the quadratic form
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}=(\gamma_{i}\cdot x)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}=(\gamma_{i}\cdot x)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
evaluating on a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum c_{i}e_{i}\in H_{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum c_{i}e_{i}\in H_{1}(\Gamma(C_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
to the square <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of the coefficient
of the edge <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in it.
See Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Example 2.15 (Degenerations of Jacobians). ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from the Mayer–Vietoris sequence that
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm gr}^{W}_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq H_{1}(C_{0}^{\nu},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>ν</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm gr}^{W}_{-1}V_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq H_{1}(C_{0}^{\nu},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}^{\nu}\to C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>ν</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}^{\nu}\to C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalization, with its natural
polarized <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-Hodge structure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.15695v3/theta_curve.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:406/269;" width="406" height="269" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Nearby fiber <math id="S2.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
to the universal deformation
<math id="S2.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon C\to\Delta^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon C\to\Delta^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of a nodal genus <math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>
curve <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with three nodes.
Vanishing cycles
<math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\gamma_{1},\gamma_{2},\gamma_{3}\}\in{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\gamma_{1},\gamma_{2},\gamma_{3}\}\in{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
shown in red, and dual graph <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(C_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(C_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
of the central fiber, shown in blue.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.16</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Degenerations of
intermediate Jacobians)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This example is due to Gwena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the unique cubic threefold with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> isolated
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> singularities,
the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Segre cubic</span>. Concretely, it is
defined by the equations</p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y_{0}\coloneqq\textstyle\left\{\sum_{i=0}^{5}x_{i}=\sum_{i=0}^{5}x_{i}^{3}=0\right\}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{5}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}\coloneqq\textstyle\left\{\sum_{i=0}^{5}x_{i}=\sum_{i=0}^{5}x_{i}^{3}=0\right\}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> singularities given by the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit
of the point <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[1:1:1:-1:-1:-1]\in{\mathbb{P}}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1:1:1:-1:-1:-1]\in{\mathbb{P}}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the
universal deformation (whose existence follows from
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, Cor. 4.2]</cite>, for example)</p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon Y\to{\rm Def}_{Y_{0}}\simeq\Delta^{10}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Def</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon Y\to{\rm Def}_{Y_{0}}\simeq\Delta^{10}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.3" class="ltx_p">of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a degeneration of smooth cubic threefolds, whose discriminant
locus is the union of the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> coordinate hyperplanes
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(u_{1}\cdots u_{10})\subset\Delta^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{1}\cdots u_{10})\subset\Delta^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similar to the universal
deformation of a nodal curve, if we label the nodes of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
and coordinate hyperplanes appropriately, then
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(u_{i})\simeq\Delta^{9}\subset\Delta^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{i})\simeq\Delta^{9}\subset\Delta^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>
is exactly the locus where the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th node is not smoothed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now define the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intermediate Jacobian fibration</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IJ\pi^{\circ}\colon IJY^{\circ}\to(\Delta^{*})^{10}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IJ\pi^{\circ}\colon IJY^{\circ}\to(\Delta^{*})^{10}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The rank <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>, polarized, unimodular <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-local system
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq(R^{3}\pi^{\circ}_{*}{\mathbb{Z}}(1))^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}_{\mathbb{Z}}\coloneqq(R^{3}\pi^{\circ}_{*}{\mathbb{Z}}(1))^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>
underlies a variation of Hodge structure of
weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> and type <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is polarized by the negation of the
intersection form on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{3}(Y_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{3}(Y_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in(\Delta^{\ast})^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in(\Delta^{\ast})^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we define <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IJY^{\circ}\coloneqq{\mathbb{V}}_{\mathbb{C}}/({\mathcal{F}}^{0}+{\mathbb{V}}_{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IJY^{\circ}\coloneqq{\mathbb{V}}_{\mathbb{C}}/({\mathcal{F}}^{0}+{\mathbb{V}}_{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}\in Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}\in Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the unique node on the general fiber
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}\to V(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}\to V(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then associated to <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cycle of
a vanishing <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}\in V_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{3}(Y_{t},{\mathbb{Z}}(-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}\in V_{\mathbb{Z}}=H_{3}(Y_{t},{\mathbb{Z}}(-1))</annotation></semantics></math>.
With an appropriate labeling
and orientations, the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> cycles <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfy the following collection
of linear relations</p>
<table id="S2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\gamma_{6}&amp;=\gamma_{5}-\gamma_{1}+\gamma_{2}\\
\gamma_{7}&amp;=\gamma_{1}-\gamma_{2}+\gamma_{3}\\
\gamma_{8}&amp;=\gamma_{2}-\gamma_{3}+\gamma_{4}\\
\gamma_{9}&amp;=\gamma_{3}-\gamma_{4}+\gamma_{5}\\
\gamma_{10}&amp;=\gamma_{4}-\gamma_{5}+\gamma_{1},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\gamma_{6}&amp;=\gamma_{5}-\gamma_{1}+\gamma_{2}\\
\gamma_{7}&amp;=\gamma_{1}-\gamma_{2}+\gamma_{3}\\
\gamma_{8}&amp;=\gamma_{2}-\gamma_{3}+\gamma_{4}\\
\gamma_{9}&amp;=\gamma_{3}-\gamma_{4}+\gamma_{5}\\
\gamma_{10}&amp;=\gamma_{4}-\gamma_{5}+\gamma_{1},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.2" class="ltx_p">see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, Sec. 7.1.10]</cite>,
and generate a primitive integral
Lagrangian subspace
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-2}\subset H_{3}(Y_{t},{\mathbb{Z}}(-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-2}\subset H_{3}(Y_{t},{\mathbb{Z}}(-1))</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. The Dehn
twist about the three-sphere <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the formula</p>
<table id="S2.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}\colon x\mapsto-(x\cdot\gamma_{i})\gamma_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}\colon x\mapsto-(x\cdot\gamma_{i})\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.3" class="ltx_p">for the logarithm of monodromy about the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate
axis. By the same computation as Example <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem15" title="Example 2.15 (Degenerations of Jacobians). ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.15</span></a>,
the monodromy bilinear forms on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}V_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfy <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}(x,x)=(\gamma_{i}\cdot x)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}(x,x)=(\gamma_{i}\cdot x)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As we will see in Remark
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Remark 4.8. ‣ 4.1. Matroids, graphs, and quadratic forms ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Matroids, graphs, and quadratic forms ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>,
there is no graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> for which
the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the edges
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and such that the relations (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Example 2.16 (Degenerations of intermediate Jacobians). ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>)
between the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by the image of the map
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{0}(\Gamma(G),{\mathbb{Z}})\to C^{1}(\Gamma(G),{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{0}(\Gamma(G),{\mathbb{Z}})\to C^{1}(\Gamma(G),{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> defined by
some choice of orientation, cf. Example <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem15" title="Example 2.15 (Degenerations of Jacobians). ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.15</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p16.1" class="ltx_p">We will give explicit extensions of
the families <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\pi^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\pi^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IJ\pi^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IJ\pi^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>
over <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>
in Examples <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem13" title="Example 4.13 (Cographic matroids). ‣ 4.2. Mumford degenerations associated to regular matroids ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem14" title="Example 4.14 (Matroidal Mumford degeneration on 𝑅̱_10). ‣ 4.2. Mumford degenerations associated to regular matroids ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a>
and in Construction <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem16" title="Construction 4.16 (Shifted matroidal degenerations). ‣ 4.3. Shifted and transversely shifted matroidal degenerations ‣ 4. Regular matroids ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.16</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Toric varieties</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall here some of the basic theory of toric
varieties. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>
for the standard notions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Toric geometry will be used both to extend
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> toroidally
(Section <a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>), and
to build Mumford degenerations
(Section <a href="#S3" title="3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
We employ the standard
toric notations of
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
for a free abelian group of rank <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}\coloneqq{\rm Hom}({\bf N},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐍</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}\coloneqq{\rm Hom}({\bf N},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
for constructions concerning the abelian and
degenerate abelian fibers.
These lattices
play, respectively, the roles of the
cocharacter and the character
lattices in toric geometry. Thus, fans lie
in <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math> while polytopes lie in <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.17</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fan</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a set of strongly convex, rational polyhedral
cones <math id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\subset{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\subset{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> for which every face
of a cone is a cone, and the intersection
of two cones is a face of each.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We do not impose the hypothesis that fans
have finitely many cones, and indeed almost
none of the fans in this paper satisfy this hypothesis.
For each cone <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> in a fan,
we may consider the <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}[\tau^{\vee}\cap{\bf M}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}[\tau^{\vee}\cap{\bf M}]</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the commutative semi-group <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\vee}\cap{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\vee}\cap{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\vee}\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\vee}\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the collection
of all linear functionals evaluating non-negatively
on <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\subset{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\subset{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We form
the corresponding affine toric variety</p>
<table id="S2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y(\tau)\coloneqq{\rm Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[\tau^{\vee}\cap{\bf M}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y(\tau)\coloneqq{\rm Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[\tau^{\vee}\cap{\bf M}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Then the gluing
<math id="S2.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathfrak{F}})\coloneqq\bigcup_{\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}}Y(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔉</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathfrak{F}})\coloneqq\bigcup_{\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}}Y(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> of these
affine schemes along the natural
open immersions corresponding face inclusions
gives the <span id="S2.SS4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">toric variety</span> <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, Sec. 1.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_notation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation 2.18</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polyhedral fan,
we notate its toric variety by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For all constructions in this paper, <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> will be an
analytic space (and even a <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme)
which is locally of finite type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As in the usual theory of toric varieties,
the torus orbits of dimension <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
isomorphic necessarily to <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
correspond bijectively
to cones of codimension <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, Sec. 3.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.19</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">polytope</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>
is a convex set in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the intersection
of a (possibly infinite) number of closed
rational half-spaces,
such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">locally of finite type</span>,
i.e. locally about every point <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in H</annotation></semantics></math>, it is
defined by a finite number of half-spaces.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.20</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">face</span>
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\subset H</annotation></semantics></math> of a polytope
is a non-empty intersection of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with a
(possibly empty) collection of supporting
hyperplanes, and the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">local monoid</span>
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of this face
is the intersection of the lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>
with the finitely
many (possibly empty)
closed half-spaces which define <math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>
in the neighborhood of
a general point of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>,
translated to the origin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">normal fan</span> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the fan formed
from the dual cones of the local monoids <math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> ranging
over all faces <math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\subset H</annotation></semantics></math> (including the open face <math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The toric variety <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=Y_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=Y_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the polytope
<math id="S2.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[{\bf M}_{F}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[{\bf M}_{F}]</annotation></semantics></math> ranging
over all faces, see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, Sec. 1.5]</cite>.
If, furthermore, all faces
of <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are integral polytopes,
then there is a canonically defined
torus-equivariant holomorphic line bundle
<math id="S2.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, given by gluing together line
bundles on each affine chart <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[{\bf M}_{F}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[{\bf M}_{F}]</annotation></semantics></math>,
in a manner which locally agrees with the recipe
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, Sec. 3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.21</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the polytope <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is compact, we may
construct <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=Y_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=Y_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>
directly as the projective variety
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{H}={\rm Proj}\,{\mathbb{C}}[{\rm Cone}(H)\cap({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Proj</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{H}={\rm Proj}\,{\mathbb{C}}[{\rm Cone}(H)\cap({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}})]</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Cone}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Cone}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is the cone over <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>,
put at height <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times\{1\}\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times\{1\}\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>; the above line bundle
is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The lattice points <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf m}\in H\cap{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf m}\in H\cap{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>
define a basis of torus-equivariant
sections of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math>.
More generally, lattice points
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf m},w)\in{\rm Cone}(H)\cap({\bf M}\times\left\{w\right\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf m},w)\in{\rm Cone}(H)\cap({\bf M}\times\left\{w\right\})</annotation></semantics></math>
of height <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to
torus-equivariant sections
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{{\bf m}/w}\in H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{{\bf m}/w}\in H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(w))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The multiplication map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(w_{1}))\otimes H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(w_{2}))\to H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(w_{1}+w_{2}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(w_{1}))\otimes H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(w_{2}))\to H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}(w_{1}+w_{2}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.2" class="ltx_p">corresponds to
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(({\bf m},w_{1}),({\bf m^{\prime}},w_{2}))\mapsto({\bf m+m^{\prime}},w_{1}+w_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(({\bf m},w_{1}),({\bf m^{\prime}},w_{2}))\mapsto({\bf m+m^{\prime}},w_{1}+w_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which
we may equivalently write as
a multiplication rule
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{{\bf m}/w_{1}}\cdot\theta_{{\bf m}^{\prime}/w_{2}}=\theta_{({\bf m}+{\bf m}^{\prime})/(w_{1}+w_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{{\bf m}/w_{1}}\cdot\theta_{{\bf m}^{\prime}/w_{2}}=\theta_{({\bf m}+{\bf m}^{\prime})/(w_{1}+w_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math>,
cf. (<a href="#S3.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) below.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose
now that there is a subgroup
<math id="S2.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\GL({\bf M})\ltimes{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\GL({\bf M})\ltimes{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> of the
integral-affine group (possibly infinite),
acting on the polytope <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a natural
action of <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, which in a
basis <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the open
torus orbit <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
by the map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{i}\mapsto c_{1}^{a_{i1}}\cdots c_{g}^{a_{ig}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\mapsto c_{1}^{a_{i1}}\cdots c_{g}^{a_{ig}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{ij})_{1\leq i,j\leq g}\in\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{ij})_{1\leq i,j\leq g}\in\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is the linear part of <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in the chosen basis.
Moreover, there is a linearization
of <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>
with respect to the <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
which acts on sections by
<math id="S2.SS4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\cdot(z^{m})=z^{a\cdot m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\cdot(z^{m})=z^{a\cdot m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-action is properly discontinuous on a tubular
neighborhood of all toric strata corresponding
to faces <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, or cones <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
whose <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-stabilizer has finite order.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.22</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A fan <math id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">regular</span>
if every cone <math id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard affine</span>; that is,
the primitive integral
generators of the extremal
rays of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> form a subset
of a basis of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, if <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal fan,
the polytope <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> should be <span id="S2.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Delzant</span>.
The toric variety
<math id="S2.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{H}=Y({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{H}=Y({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth analytic space if and only
if <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is regular, in which case for each
<math id="S2.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the affine toric variety <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>,
see (<a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 2.4. Toric varieties ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), is isomorphic to a product of an
affine space with a torus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.23</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem23.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.Thmtheorem23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">support</span> (in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>)
of a fan <math id="S2.Thmtheorem23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of all
cones <math id="S2.Thmtheorem23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, a <span id="S2.SS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">morphism</span> of fans <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}\to{\mathfrak{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔊</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}\to{\mathfrak{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a linear map between the corresponding cocharacter
lattices, which sends cones into cones.
It induces a torus-equivariant map
<math id="S2.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathfrak{F}})\to Y({\mathfrak{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathfrak{F}})\to Y({\mathfrak{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5. </span>Toroidal extensions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now outline the
construction of toroidal extensions of <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> associated
to a monodromy cone <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and more generally a fan <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>
for references on toroidal compactifications of
Siegel spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.24</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.Thmtheorem24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fan</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a rational polyhedral fan, whose support
is contained in
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and
such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant
under the action <math id="S2.Thmtheorem24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\mapsto ABA^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\mapsto ABA^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>,
with finitely many orbits of cones.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.25</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
be an abelian fibration with unipotent
monodromies about the coordinate hyperplanes.
Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}_{f}\coloneqq\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔉</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}_{f}\coloneqq\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a fan,
when <math id="S2.Thmtheorem25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> injects
into <math id="S2.Thmtheorem25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>trop</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a polyhedral cone which embeds
into <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>trop</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}</annotation></semantics></math>. In what follows, we will
consider the fan <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the coordinate-wise exponential mapping:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E\colon{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E\colon{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\sigma_{ij})_{i,j=1}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>g</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\sigma_{ij})_{i,j=1}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto(\exp(2\pi i\sigma_{ij}))_{i,j=1}^{g}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>g</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto(\exp(2\pi i\sigma_{ij}))_{i,j=1}^{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient by the action
of translation by <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>
defines an open embedding of the quotient
of Siegel space (Def. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 2.3. ‣ 2.2. Principally polarized abelian varieties ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>)
into a torus</p>
<table id="S2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{E}\colon U_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\hookrightarrow U_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}})\simeq\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{\ast})\simeq{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq U_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{E}\colon U_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\hookrightarrow U_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}})\simeq\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{\ast})\simeq{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq U_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset P_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset P_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the unipotent radical of the parabolic,
as in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>,
and the isomorphism
<math id="S2.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{\ast}\simeq\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{\ast}\simeq\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by
<math id="S2.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n_{ij})_{1\leq i,j\leq g}\otimes\lambda=(\lambda^{n_{ij}})_{1\leq i,j\leq g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n_{ij})_{1\leq i,j\leq g}\otimes\lambda=(\lambda^{n_{ij}})_{1\leq i,j\leq g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is called the
<span id="S2.SS5.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">first</span> or <span id="S2.SS5.p2.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unipotent partial quotient</span>
of <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> in the theory of
toroidal compactifications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Compose the period maps
<math id="S2.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>nilp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}</annotation></semantics></math>
of any degeneration with <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}={\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}={\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>
with the quotient map <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\to\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\to\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
They descend to single-valued maps
<math id="S2.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi,\Phi_{\rm{nilp}}\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>nilp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi,\Phi_{\rm{nilp}}\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Composing <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\rm nilp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>nilp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\rm nilp}</annotation></semantics></math> with the map
<math id="S2.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{E}</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to a map</p>
<table id="S2.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq\Sym_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">whose image is (an analytic open subset of)
a translate of the
subtorus <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\coloneqq({\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}})\cap{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\coloneqq({\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}})\cap{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 2.9. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> can be rephrased as
saying that the period mapping
is approximated by a translate of a subtorus,
near <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since the fan <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> sits in the
co-character lattice <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
the associated toric variety <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a toroidal
extension of <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the quotient <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash Y({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash Y({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This quotient is not globally well-behaved, e.g. the action
of <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> fixes the origin point of the open torus orbit.
But there is a tubular analytic neighborhood
<math id="S2.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathfrak{F}})\subset Y({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathfrak{F}})\subset Y({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> of the union of the toric
boundary strata of <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponding to cones intersecting <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> on which
the <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>-action is properly discontinuous.
Let <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the intersection of <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> with
the open torus orbit <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we have open embeddings</p>
<table id="S2.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash T({\mathfrak{F}})\hookleftarrow\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash T^{*}({\mathfrak{F}})\hookrightarrow P_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↩</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash T({\mathfrak{F}})\hookleftarrow\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash T^{*}({\mathfrak{F}})\hookrightarrow P_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Thm. 4.9(iv)]</cite>,
the boundary of <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally
modeled near the Baily-Borel cusp (associated to the
Lagrangian subspace <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}e_{1}\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}e_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}e_{1}\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}e_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>) by <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, by (<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), we may glue
<math id="S2.SS5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash T({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash T({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S2.SS5.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}=\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}=\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> along their
common analytic open subset <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash T^{*}({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash T^{*}({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, the same construction applies
to any <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant fan <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the resulting toroidal extension
is relatively proper over the <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional
cusp of the Baily-Borel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>
compactification <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\rm BB}={\mathcal{A}}_{g}\sqcup{\mathcal{A}}_{g-1}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup{\mathcal{A}}_{1}\sqcup{\mathcal{A}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>BB</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊔</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\rm BB}={\mathcal{A}}_{g}\sqcup{\mathcal{A}}_{g-1}\sqcup\cdots\sqcup{\mathcal{A}}_{1}\sqcup{\mathcal{A}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
if and only if <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Supp}({\mathfrak{F}})={\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Supp}({\mathfrak{F}})={\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains cones
supported in <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\setminus{\mathcal{P}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\setminus{\mathcal{P}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the gluing defined by (<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>)
further extends along the
intermediate-dimensional strata of the
Baily-Borel compactification.
This is the <span id="S2.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">toroidal extension</span>
<math id="S2.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathfrak{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔉</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathfrak{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the orbifold <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Supp}({\mathfrak{F}})={\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Supp}({\mathfrak{F}})={\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, we notate
the toroidal extension by
<math id="S2.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔉</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>;
it is proper, and we call it a
<span id="S2.SS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">toroidal compactification</span>.
It follows
from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Thm. 5.2]</cite>
that <math id="S2.SS5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔉</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔉</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a DM algebraic stack,
in the former case
by refining and extending <math id="S2.SS5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a
fan with full support <math id="S2.SS5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_notation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation 2.26</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem26.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For simplicity,
we will write <math id="S2.Thmtheorem26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> to notate the toroidal
extension associated to the fan
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting
of the orbit of a polyhedral cone <math id="S2.Thmtheorem26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.27</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any degeneration of PPAVs <math id="S2.Thmtheorem27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\ast}\colon X^{\ast}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\ast}\colon X^{\ast}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
with monodromy cone <math id="S2.Thmtheorem27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the period map
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a unique
holomorphic extension
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem27.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proposition follows from Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 2.9. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>: We have shown
that the nilpotent orbit <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\rm nilp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>nilp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\rm nilp}</annotation></semantics></math> maps,
analytically-locally near the boundary of <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>,
into the translate of
the subtorus <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, see (<a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
Any cocharacter
<math id="S2.SS5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes({\mathbb{C}}\setminus 0)\subset\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes({\mathbb{C}}\setminus 0)\subset\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
for <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, admits a completion over <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
to the toroidal extension
<math id="S2.SS5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, sending <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>
into the toroidal boundary stratum corresponding to the
cone of <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in its relative interior.
We deduce that <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\rm nilp}\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>nilp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\rm nilp}\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
extends holomorphically to a map
<math id="S2.SS5.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, it follows
from the exponential convergence (<a href="#S2.I2.i2" title="Item 2 ‣ Theorem 2.9. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)
of <math id="S2.SS5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> towards <math id="S2.SS5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\rm nilp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>nilp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\rm nilp}</annotation></semantics></math> (in
the invariant metric on <math id="S2.SS5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>) that
<math id="S2.SS5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\colon(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
admits a continuous extension <math id="S2.SS5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is normal, Riemann’s removable singularities theorem
implies that this continuous extension is holomorphic.
It is unique because the toroidal extension is a separated
analytic stack.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.28</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, one may wish to consider
monodromy cones <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> for which the
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> does not
embed into <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>trop</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{A}}_{g})_{\rm trop}</annotation></semantics></math> (Def. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem14" title="Definition 2.14. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a>).
The issue here is that
the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of such a cone may intersect itself.
This problem is resolved by rather quotienting
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\subset U_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\subset U_{\mathbb{Z}}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
by a finite index subgroup <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For instance, consider the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-torsion subgroup
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X[n]\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[n]\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{2g}</annotation></semantics></math> of a PPAV <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
The principal polarization
defines a natural non-degenerate symplectic pairing
on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[n]</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the moduli space
of abelian varieties <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with
<span id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">full Lagrangian level <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> structure</span>
by adding the data of:</p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a Lagrangian subspace
<math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{g}\subset X[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi\simeq({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{g}\subset X[n]</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-basis of <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All degenerations we consider in this paper admit
some full Lagrangian level <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> structure, because
there is a distinguished Lagrangian subspace
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> on which the monodromy
acts trivially. The moduli stack <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>
of abelian varieties
with full Lagrangian level <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> structure is
an étale cover <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}={\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}={\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At an appropriate <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-cusp of the Baily–Borel compactification
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> (the cusp corresponding to a
Lagrangian subspace
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Xi}\subset H_{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Xi}\subset H_{1}(X,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> for which
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{n}\widetilde{\Xi}/\widetilde{\Xi}=\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{n}\widetilde{\Xi}/\widetilde{\Xi}=\Xi</annotation></semantics></math>),
the parabolic stabilizer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> has the following structure:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to U_{\mathbb{Z}}\to P_{\mathbb{Z}}\to\Gamma(n)\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to U_{\mathbb{Z}}\to P_{\mathbb{Z}}\to\Gamma(n)\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where the unipotent subgroup
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}\simeq{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is the same as without level structure, but
the Levi quotient
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(n)\coloneqq\ker(\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\to\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(n)\coloneqq\ker(\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\to\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}}))</annotation></semantics></math>
is the full level <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> subgroup.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then, a toroidal extension of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
at this Baily–Borel cusp has the “advantage” that
a fan need only be <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(n)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant. Furthermore,
the fundamental domain for the action of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(n)</annotation></semantics></math>
is larger than the fundamental domain for that action
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, given any polyhedral
cone <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists some <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
(depending on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>) for which <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> embeds into the
quotient <math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(n)\backslash{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(n)\backslash{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The preceding results also apply at this cusp, since
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem28.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(n)\cdot{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(n)\cdot{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> now defines a fan.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.29</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a strictly
convex, rational polyhedral cone, and
consider any homomorphism
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
for which
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\coloneq\phi_{\mathbb{R}}(\tau)\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\coloneq\phi_{\mathbb{R}}(\tau)\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
There exists a quasiprojective
variety <math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, divisor <math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
point <math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in D</annotation></semantics></math>, and projective
family <math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{*}=Y\setminus D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}=Y\setminus D</annotation></semantics></math>,
of PPAVs of dimension <math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, in
the algebraic category, whose
monodromy cone at <math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem29.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>
in the following sense:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> admits, near </span><math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, an étale-local
isomorphism to the toric variety </span><math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
sending </span><math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to the toric boundary, and </span><math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
to the torus fixed point.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the monodromy representation of </span><math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\setminus D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\setminus D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> near </span><math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is given, under this isomorphism, by</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}({\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*},*)\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\xrightarrow{\phi}{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})=U_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset{\rm Sp}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="italic">ϕ</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}({\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*},*)\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\xrightarrow{\phi}{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})=U_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset{\rm Sp}_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here </span><math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\subset Y(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\subset Y(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is
the open torus orbit.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that it follows
from (<a href="#S2.I4.i1" title="Item 1 ‣ Proposition 2.29. ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) that <math id="S2.SS5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
is smooth near <math id="S2.SS5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first prove the proposition
under the hypothesis that <math id="S2.SS5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is
injective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the étale cover
<math id="S2.SS5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
given by full Lagrangian level <math id="S2.SS5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>
structure as in Remark <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem28" title="Remark 2.28. ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.28</span></a>.
As noted, there is a Baily–Borel cusp
of <math id="S2.SS5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]</annotation></semantics></math> whose parabolic
stabilizer <math id="S2.SS5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> has unipotent subgroup
<math id="S2.SS5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
which is the same as for <math id="S2.SS5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> but whose
Levi quotient is
<math id="S2.SS5.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathbb{Z}}/U_{\mathbb{Z}}=\Gamma(n)\subset\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathbb{Z}}/U_{\mathbb{Z}}=\Gamma(n)\subset\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.SS5.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is neat for <math id="S2.SS5.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>,
its action on the toroidal extension
<math id="S2.SS5.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]^{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝔉</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]^{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is free
in the tubular neighborhood
<math id="S2.SS5.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathfrak{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathfrak{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).
We deduce that there is a Zariski open
<math id="S2.SS5.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]^{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝔉</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]^{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
containing
all maximally degenerate strata,
over which
the universal family
<math id="S2.SS5.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}|_{V^{*}}\to{V^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}|_{V^{*}}\to{V^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> of PPAVs,
<math id="S2.SS5.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{*}\coloneqq V\cap{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{*}\coloneqq V\cap{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>,
exists as a scheme (rather than just
a DM stack).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then, choose <math id="S2.SS5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> so that
<math id="S2.SS5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the strict interior of a
fundamental domain for the action of
<math id="S2.SS5.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(n)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS5.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and define
<math id="S2.SS5.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}=\Gamma(n)\cdot{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}=\Gamma(n)\cdot{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the
construction of toroidal extensions,
the open set
<math id="S2.SS5.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is étale-locally isomorphic
to the toric variety
<math id="S2.SS5.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Choose
a finite index sublattice
<math id="S2.SS5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
for which <math id="S2.SS5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\cap{\mathbb{B}}={\rm im}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\cap{\mathbb{B}}={\rm im}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the finite étale cover
<math id="S2.SS5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
of algebraic tori
induces a branched cover of toric
varieties <math id="S2.SS5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\Lambda\otimes{\mathbb{R}}}(\tau)\to Y_{{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\Lambda\otimes{\mathbb{R}}}(\tau)\to Y_{{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Take an algebraic branched
cover <math id="S2.SS5.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}\to V</annotation></semantics></math> with the same
branching over the toric boundary,
under the étale-local identification
of <math id="S2.SS5.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]^{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝔉</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}[n]^{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S2.SS5.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S2.SS5.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is étale-locally
isomorphic to <math id="S2.SS5.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\Lambda\otimes{\mathbb{R}}}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\Lambda\otimes{\mathbb{R}}}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>,
over the deepest toroidal stratum.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, to construct <math id="S2.SS5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
we slice <math id="S2.SS5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S2.SS5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{g+1\choose 2}-\dim\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{g+1\choose 2}-\dim\tau</annotation></semantics></math>
generic hyperplanes, which under
the étale-local identification of <math id="S2.SS5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S2.SS5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\Lambda\otimes{\mathbb{R}}}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\Lambda\otimes{\mathbb{R}}}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> are
transversal to the deepest toroidal
boundary stratum. We set <math id="S2.SS5.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
to be the inverse image of <math id="S2.SS5.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.SS5.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=Y\setminus Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=Y\setminus Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS5.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in D</annotation></semantics></math>
as an intersection point
of the hyperplanes with the deepest
stratum. We set the family
of abelian varieties
<math id="S2.SS5.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to be
the pullback of the universal family
<math id="S2.SS5.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}|_{V^{*}}\to V^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}|_{V^{*}}\to V^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (which exists
by the above discussion)
along
the map <math id="S2.SS5.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{*}\to V^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{*}\to V^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">To verify that the monodromy
representation of <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is as specified,
consider a <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter
family <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{*}\to Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{*}\to Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
which extends to a map
<math id="S2.SS5.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> sending <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\mapsto 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\mapsto 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The monodromy over <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
can be computed in the étale-local
model as the monodromy of the family
of PPAVs over a co-character.
Such co-characters correspond
to lattice points, in <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\cap{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\cap{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
is pulled back from <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the monodromy representation is pulled back along
the morphism of cocharacter
lattices, given by the inclusion <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\hookrightarrow{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\hookrightarrow{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> which, in particular, restricts
<math id="S2.SS5.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, it suffices
to observe that the monodromy over
the cocharacter <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically identified
with <math id="S2.SS5.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\cap{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\cap{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
see e.g. (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we address the case
where <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is not injective.
Define
<math id="S2.SS5.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\tau}:=\phi_{\mathbb{R}}(\tau)\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\tau}:=\phi_{\mathbb{R}}(\tau)\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, viewed as a polyhedral cone
in the lattice <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi({\mathbb{Z}}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi({\mathbb{Z}}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have a descended, injective homomorphism
<math id="S2.SS5.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\phi}\colon\phi({\mathbb{Z}}^{k})\hookrightarrow{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\phi}\colon\phi({\mathbb{Z}}^{k})\hookrightarrow{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying the hypotheses of
the proposition. Thus, there is a
family <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f}^{*}\colon{\overline{X}}^{*}\to{\overline{Y}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f}^{*}\colon{\overline{X}}^{*}\to{\overline{Y}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
over a base <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{Y}}^{*}={\overline{Y}}\setminus{\overline{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{Y}}^{*}={\overline{Y}}\setminus{\overline{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
a point <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{0}\in{\overline{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{0}\in{\overline{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and an étale-local isomorphism
<math id="S2.SS5.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon{\overline{Y}}\to Y(\overline{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon{\overline{Y}}\to Y(\overline{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> near
<math id="S2.SS5.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{0}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
the conclusion of the proposition.
Then the base change of the morphism
<math id="S2.SS5.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\circ\overline{f}^{*}\colon{\overline{X}}^{*}\to Y(\overline{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\circ\overline{f}^{*}\colon{\overline{X}}^{*}\to Y(\overline{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> along
the morphism of
toric varieties <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(\tau)\to Y(\overline{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\tau)\to Y(\overline{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>
produces the desired family
<math id="S2.SS5.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}:={\overline{Y}}^{*}\times_{Y(\overline{\tau})}Y(\tau)\subset{\overline{Y}}\times_{Y(\overline{\tau})}Y(\tau)\eqqcolon Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≕</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}:={\overline{Y}}^{*}\times_{Y(\overline{\tau})}Y(\tau)\subset{\overline{Y}}\times_{Y(\overline{\tau})}Y(\tau)\eqqcolon Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here we take <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> to be the fiber
product of the points <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{0}\in{\overline{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{0}\in{\overline{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and the torus fixed point of <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the desired monodromy
map factors through <math id="S2.SS5.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the condition on monodromy follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.30</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\cap{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\cap{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is a smooth quasiprojective
variety <math id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, an snc divisor
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math>, a zero-stratum
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in D</annotation></semantics></math>, and
a projective algebraic family
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\colon X^{*}\to Y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of PPAVs,
such that the local monodromy
bilinear form (Def. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Definition 2.6. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>)
about the component <math id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}\ni 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∋</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}\ni 0</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem30.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.SS5.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be primitive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">The corollary follows
from Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem29" title="Proposition 2.29. ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.29</span></a>,
by taking
<math id="S2.SS5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the homomorphism
<math id="S2.SS5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
sending <math id="S2.SS5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\mapsto B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\mapsto B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here one can assume that <math id="S2.SS5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth
and <math id="S2.SS5.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is snc because
they are étale-locally isomorphic
to <math id="S2.SS5.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(\tau)={\mathbb{C}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\tau)={\mathbb{C}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the union
of the coordinate hyperplanes, respectively.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.31</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given any two families <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of
PPAVs over <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the same integral monodromies
about each coordinate axis, there is an analytic deformation
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}^{*}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}\times Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}^{*}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}\times Z</annotation></semantics></math> of such families,
over a connected base <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and points <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,2\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,2\in Z</annotation></semantics></math> for which
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}^{*}_{i}\simeq X^{*}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}^{*}_{i}\simeq X^{*}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, we may
first deform each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to the nilpotent orbit
(<a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) passing
through the same point of the deepest toroidal stratum
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and then relate the two
translates of subtori <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
by a translation in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem31.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>The Mumford construction</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, we have shown
how to extend <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> toroidally, to obtain <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}\hookrightarrow{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\hookrightarrow\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\hookrightarrow\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>),
so that
the period map <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{*})^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
of any degeneration <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
with monodromy cone <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{f}={\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{f}={\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits an extension
of the period map
over the punctures <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will now describe how to extend the
universal family of PPAVs over <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> so that we may
pull back this extension to produce particularly
nice birational models of degenerations.
It is useful to have examples in mind;
many are provided in Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
All degenerations we consider
in this section are maximal, in the sense
of Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="Definition 2.13. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Mumford construction, fan version</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be a
positive-definite, symmetric, integral,
bilinear form on a lattice <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> defines a homomorphism</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\colon{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\colon{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\to{\bf M}^{\vee}={\bf N}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\to{\bf M}^{\vee}={\bf N},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bf m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bf m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto B({\bf m},-)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto B({\bf m},-).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}\subset{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}\subset{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm im}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm im}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In terms of symmetric <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\times g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\times g</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the span of the rows (or columns) of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and so
defines a finite index sublattice of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\rm trop}(B)\coloneqq{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>trop</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\rm trop}(B)\coloneqq{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tropical abelian variety</span>
associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A (resp. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tiling</span>
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\rm trop}(B)={\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>trop</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\rm trop}(B)={\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a decomposition into convex polytopes with
integer (resp. rational) vertices,
or equivalently, a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic
(resp. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-)polytopal tesselation of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">complete triangulation</span> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\rm trop}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>trop</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\rm trop}(B)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a tiling, all of
whose polytopes are lattice simplices
of minimal volume <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/g!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/g!)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter case)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be
positive-definite and let
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-tiling of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\rm trop}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>trop</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\rm trop}(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter
Mumford degeneration associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Embed <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\simeq{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times\{1\}\hookrightarrow{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\simeq{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times\{1\}\hookrightarrow{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>
as an affine hyperplane at height <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>
in a space of one dimension higher.
Then, the cone over the tiling
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> defines a rational
polyhedral fan in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}^{g+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}^{g+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
See Figure <a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Example 3.34 (Theta graph, 1-parameter). ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>
for an example of a tiling
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2}/\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2}/\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;1\\
1&amp;3\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>3</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;1\\
1&amp;3\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corresponding
infinite type toric variety. The action of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> by translations on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a
linear action <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}\hookrightarrow\GL_{g+1}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}\hookrightarrow\GL_{g+1}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>
acting on the fan <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and hence induces an action of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))</annotation></semantics></math> by automorphisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that the height function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by
projecting to the final coordinate,
defines a morphism of fans
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})\to{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})\to{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the fan of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
(which is simply the positive ray in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Hence, there is an induced
map of toric varieties</p>
<table id="S3.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to Y({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})={\mathbb{C}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to Y({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})={\mathbb{C}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Since the action of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the height function,
this morphism descends to the quotient
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}\backslash Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}\backslash Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, though
this full quotient is poorly behaved. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>
be the monomial coordinate about <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By standard toric geometry, we have:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fiber of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> over
<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}={\mathbb{C}}\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}={\mathbb{C}}\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the fiber
over <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=0\in{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=0\in{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. the toric boundary)
is an infinite quilt of complete toric varieties,
whose dual complex is the original tiling <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the dual complex <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the toric boundary by translation, and
acts on the fiber over <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> by translations by
the rank <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> subgroup
<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{B}\coloneqq\Lambda_{B}\otimes u\subset{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{B}\coloneqq\Lambda_{B}\otimes u\subset{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
which in coordinates is the subgroup</p>
<table id="S3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle(u^{B_{11}},\dots,u^{B_{1g}}),\,\dots,\,(u^{B_{g1}},\dots,u^{B_{gg}})\rangle\subset({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g},\quad B=(B_{ij})\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle(u^{B_{11}},\dots,u^{B_{1g}}),\,\dots,\,(u^{B_{g1}},\dots,u^{B_{gg}})\rangle\subset({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g},\quad B=(B_{ij})\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then, since <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is positive-definite,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{B}\subset{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{B}\subset{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a discrete
subgroup for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\Delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\Delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence the action of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}\simeq\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}\simeq\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is properly
discontinuous over the unit disk
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, because
the action is clearly properly discontinuous
over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely on the tiles of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Setting <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\coloneqq\Lambda_{B}\backslash Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))^{u\in\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\coloneqq\Lambda_{B}\backslash Y({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))^{u\in\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get
a proper, complex-analytic degeneration</p>
<table id="S3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\Delta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">of complex tori, called the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter
Mumford degeneration</span> associated to the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-tiling
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\rm trop}(B)={\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>trop</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\rm trop}(B)={\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The general fiber is principally polarized,
by a symplectic form <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> defined as follows:
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\Delta^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\Delta^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, noting that we have a canonical isomorphism
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}({\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*},{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}({\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*},{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we
have a canonical exact sequence</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\to{\bf N}\to H_{1}(X_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})\xrightarrow{\sigma}{\bf M}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐍</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜎</mo></mover><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\to{\bf N}\to H_{1}(X_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})\xrightarrow{\sigma}{\bf M}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">induced by the long exact sequence of homotopy
groups associated to the fibration</p>
<table id="S3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{B}\hookrightarrow{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{\ast}\to{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{\ast}/u^{B}=X_{u}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>B</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{B}\hookrightarrow{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{\ast}\to{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{\ast}/u^{B}=X_{u}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Here we use that we
have canonical isomorphisms
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{B}\simeq\Lambda_{B}\simeq{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{B}\simeq\Lambda_{B}\simeq{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we may define a unimodular
symplectic form on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(X_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(X_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> by
choosing a splitting of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and then
using the canonical pairing between
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The splitting of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
we choose is specified by a
choice of logarithm <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi i\tau=\log u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi i\tau=\log u</annotation></semantics></math>.
Such a choice
gives
a presentation</p>
<table id="S3.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{u}={\bf N}_{\mathbb{C}}/({\bf N}\oplus\tau N({\bf M}))\simeq{\mathbb{C}}^{g}/({\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\oplus\tau B({\mathbb{Z}}^{g}));" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{u}={\bf N}_{\mathbb{C}}/({\bf N}\oplus\tau N({\bf M}))\simeq{\mathbb{C}}^{g}/({\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\oplus\tau B({\mathbb{Z}}^{g}));</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.4" class="ltx_p">the resulting
symplectic form <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is then independent
of the choice of logarithm <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>
because the transformation
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\mapsto\tau+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\mapsto\tau+1</annotation></semantics></math>
defines a symplectomorphism of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(X_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(X_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, the symplectic form <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>
is given as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(({\bf n},{\bf m}),({\bf n}^{\prime},{\bf m}^{\prime}))={\bf m}^{\prime}({\bf n})-{\bf m}({\bf n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(({\bf n},{\bf m}),({\bf n}^{\prime},{\bf m}^{\prime}))={\bf m}^{\prime}({\bf n})-{\bf m}({\bf n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, for
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf n},{\bf m}),({\bf n}^{\prime},{\bf m}^{\prime})\in{\bf M}\oplus\bf N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf n},{\bf m}),({\bf n}^{\prime},{\bf m}^{\prime})\in{\bf M}\oplus\bf N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, given a symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\times g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\times g</annotation></semantics></math> matrix
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=(a_{ij})\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=(a_{ij})\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, we may
perform the same construction, but instead quotient
by the subgroup</p>
<table id="S3.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="au^{B}\coloneqq\langle(a_{11}u^{B_{11}},\dots,a_{1g}u^{B_{1g}}),\,\dots,\,(a_{g1}u^{B_{g1}},\dots,a_{gg}u^{B_{gg}})\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">au^{B}\coloneqq\langle(a_{11}u^{B_{11}},\dots,a_{1g}u^{B_{1g}}),\,\dots,\,(a_{g1}u^{B_{g1}},\dots,a_{gg}u^{B_{gg}})\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">We must take <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|u|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|u|</annotation></semantics></math> small enough that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-B\log|u|-\log|a_{ij}|&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-B\log|u|-\log|a_{ij}|&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then this construction may be performed relatively,
choosing <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in
some subtorus of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>
forming coset representatives
for the natural action of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We get an analytic degeneration</p>
<table id="S3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}\colon X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\Delta^{\rm univ}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}\colon X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\Delta^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\rm univ}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\rm univ}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a holomorphic disk
bundle over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>
restricts to a family of PPAVs over the punctured
disc bundle
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{\ast})^{\rm{univ}}\subset\Delta^{\rm{univ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{\ast})^{\rm{univ}}\subset\Delta^{\rm{univ}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of the toroidal
extensions of Section <a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math>
maps to the toroidal extension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathfrak{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔉</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}^{{\mathfrak{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
whose fan is (the orbit of) a single
ray <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{F}}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{F}}=\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})\cdot{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B</annotation></semantics></math>.
So every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter
family
of PPAVs with monodromy
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> admits an extension pulled back from
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>
along an arc transverse to the boundary divisor
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0\}\times{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}=\Delta^{\rm univ}\setminus(\Delta^{*})^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>univ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0\}\times{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})/{\mathbb{C}}^{*}=\Delta^{\rm univ}\setminus(\Delta^{*})^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\clubsuit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">♣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\clubsuit</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Allowing the tiling
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> to have strictly rational vertices corresponds
to allowing the components of the central
fiber to have non-reduced components.
More precisely, the irreducible components
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}\subset X_{0}({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\coloneqq f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}\subset X_{0}({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\coloneqq f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>
are in bijection with the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-cells
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\in{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\in{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the multiplicity <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the smallest positive integer
for which <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}v_{i}\in{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}v_{i}\in{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> has
integral vertices, the total space <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))</annotation></semantics></math> of the Mumford
degeneration is smooth if
and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete triangulation,
i.e. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a tiling by standard lattice simplices.
This condition ensures that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is a regular fan—its cones are
all standard affine cones.
Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a
semistable,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-trivial degeneration (with smooth total space),
see Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 3.14. ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a> below.
These are sometimes called Kulikov models,
in analogy to K3 degenerations.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By passing to an
intermediate cover <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
as in Remark <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem28" title="Remark 2.28. ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.28</span></a>, we may assume
that the Levi quotient
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>GL</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset\GL_{g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
of the parabolic stabilizer
at some Baily–Borel <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-cusp of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
acts freely on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, when <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive,
the boundary divisor of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B}</annotation></semantics></math>
is isomorphic to the discriminant
divisor of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather
than a further finite quotient of it. In this case, the base
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> glues onto <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and thus,
we may extend
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> to a family
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}\colon X^{\rm univ}({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}\colon X^{\rm univ}({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B}</annotation></semantics></math>
extending the universal family
over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A priori,
this extension exists
only in the category of analytic spaces.
But it is always an algebraic space
(Prop. <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6.2. Algebraicity and projectivity ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>),
and for certain choices of tiling <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we may ensure it is relatively projective,
see Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is not primitive, the base <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>
rather maps to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B}</annotation></semantics></math>
by a map ramified over the toroidal boundary divisor,
to order the imprimitivity of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The standard <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter
Mumford degeneration <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X({\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}}))\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponding to a tiling <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> has monodromy invariant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
The period map on the universal cover of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the nilpotent orbit through the origin:
The maps</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi},\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm{nilp}}\colon{\mathbb{H}}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>nilp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℍ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi},\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm{nilp}}\colon{\mathbb{H}}\to{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">satisfy
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau)=\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau)=\tau B," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>nilp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau)=\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau)=\tau B,</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=e^{2\pi i\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=e^{2\pi i\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is the coordinate
on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. More generally, the period map
on the restriction of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> to
the universal cover of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{*})^{\rm univ}|_{\{a\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{*})^{\rm univ}|_{\{a\}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>,
is given by
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau)=\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau)=\tfrac{1}{2\pi i}(\log a_{ij})+\tau B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>nilp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}(\tau)=\widetilde{\Phi}_{\rm nilp}(\tau)=\tfrac{1}{2\pi i}(\log a_{ij})+\tau B</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">On the one hand, the coordinatewise
exponential <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\tau B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\tau B)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}\exp(2\pi iB_{11}\tau)&amp;\cdots&amp;\exp(2\pi iB_{1g}\tau)\\
\cdots&amp;&amp;\cdots\\
\exp(2\pi iB_{g1}\tau)&amp;\cdots&amp;\exp(2\pi iB_{gg}\tau)\\
\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}u^{B_{11}}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{B_{1g}}\\
\cdots&amp;&amp;\cdots\\
u^{B_{g1}}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{B_{gg}}\\
\end{pmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}\exp(2\pi iB_{11}\tau)&amp;\cdots&amp;\exp(2\pi iB_{1g}\tau)\\
\cdots&amp;&amp;\cdots\\
\exp(2\pi iB_{g1}\tau)&amp;\cdots&amp;\exp(2\pi iB_{gg}\tau)\\
\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}u^{B_{11}}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{B_{1g}}\\
\cdots&amp;&amp;\cdots\\
u^{B_{g1}}&amp;\cdots&amp;u^{B_{gg}}\\
\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">On the other hand, the fiber of the universal family
<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> over the period matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in{\mathcal{H}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in{\mathcal{H}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
is simply the complex torus
<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{g}/({\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\sigma)\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}/\langle\textrm{rows of }E(\sigma)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mtext>rows of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{g}/({\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\sigma)\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}/\langle\textrm{rows of }E(\sigma)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>
so the proposition follows.∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter case)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now extend Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Construction 3.3 (1-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> to the multivariable
setting. Consider a collection of positive semi-definite
bilinear forms <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math>
for which <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{k}B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{k}B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive-definite.
These bilinear forms define
a collection of symmetric homomorphisms
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}\colon{\bf M}\to{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}\colon{\bf M}\to{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the quotient of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}\simeq({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
by the subgroup</p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle u_{1}^{B_{1}}\cdots u_{k}^{B_{k}}\coloneqq\langle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{1}^{B_{1}}\cdots u_{k}^{B_{k}}\coloneqq\langle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex46.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(u_{1}^{(B_{1})_{11}}\cdots u_{k}^{(B_{r})_{11}},\dots,u_{1}^{(B_{1})_{1g}}\cdots u_{k}^{(B_{k})_{1g}}),\,\dots\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>11</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>11</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u_{1}^{(B_{1})_{11}}\cdots u_{k}^{(B_{r})_{11}},\dots,u_{1}^{(B_{1})_{1g}}\cdots u_{k}^{(B_{k})_{1g}}),\,\dots\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(u_{1}^{(B_{1})_{g1}}\cdots u_{k}^{(B_{k})_{g1}},\dots,u_{1}^{(B_{1})_{gg}}\cdots u_{k}^{(B_{k})_{gg}})\rangle\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(u_{1}^{(B_{1})_{g1}}\cdots u_{k}^{(B_{k})_{g1}},\dots,u_{1}^{(B_{1})_{gg}}\cdots u_{k}^{(B_{k})_{gg}})\rangle\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|u_{i}|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|u_{i}|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the resulting action of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{1}^{B_{1}}\cdots u_{k}^{B_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}^{B_{1}}\cdots u_{k}^{B_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is
properly discontinuous. Thus,
the quotient is a fibration</p>
<table id="S3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{\ast}\colon X^{*}(B_{1},\dots,B_{k})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{\ast}\colon X^{*}(B_{1},\dots,B_{k})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2.3" class="ltx_p">of PPAVs, over a punctured polydisk,
where the base has coordinates <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To define
an extension over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we require a fan <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>
inside of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}^{g+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}^{g+k}</annotation></semantics></math>
which is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic
for an action respecting the projection to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
More precisely, declare <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf m}\in{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf m}\in{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>
to act linearly on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
by</p>
<table id="S3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle({\bf n},\,\vec{r})\mapsto({\bf n}+(\vec{r}\cdot\vec{N})({\bf m}),\,\vec{r})\in{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐧</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle({\bf n},\,\vec{r})\mapsto({\bf n}+(\vec{r}\cdot\vec{N})({\bf m}),\,\vec{r})\in{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{r}\cdot\vec{N}\coloneqq r_{1}N_{1}+\cdots+r_{k}N_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{r}\cdot\vec{N}\coloneqq r_{1}N_{1}+\cdots+r_{k}N_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}\colon{\bf M}\to{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}\colon{\bf M}\to{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>
are the symmetric homomorphisms
associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> as above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The fan <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> must then be <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic
(with respect to the action (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>))
and the projection
to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> must induce a morphism of fans to
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Supp}({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Supp}({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{g}\times(1,\dots,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{g}\times(1,\dots,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, we usually require that the fan
morphism
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}\to({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}\to({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">flat</span>,
that is, the image
of any cone of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> intersected
with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
cone of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> intersected
with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then the multivariable Mumford construction is the result
of quotienting by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> the inverse
image <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathcal{S}})^{u\in\Delta^{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathcal{S}})^{u\in\Delta^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in
the infinite type toric variety <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We call this quotient</p>
<table id="S3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f\colon X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f\colon X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.2" class="ltx_p">It is a proper, analytic,
flat extension of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\ast}\colon X^{*}(B_{1},\dots,B_{k})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\ast}\colon X^{*}(B_{1},\dots,B_{k})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
with flatness guaranteed by the
flatness of the fan map.
As in Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Construction 3.3 (1-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>,
the fibration <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bf M}=u_{1}^{B_{1}}\cdots u_{k}^{B_{k}}\hookrightarrow{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{\ast}\to X_{u}\coloneqq f^{-1}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}=u_{1}^{B_{1}}\cdots u_{k}^{B_{k}}\hookrightarrow{\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{\ast}\to X_{u}\coloneqq f^{-1}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>
defines an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\to{\bf N}\to H_{1}(X_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})\xrightarrow{\sigma}{\bf M}\to 0,\quad\quad u=(u_{1},\dotsc,u_{k})\in(\Delta^{\ast})^{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐍</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜎</mo></mover><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\to{\bf N}\to H_{1}(X_{u},{\mathbb{Z}})\xrightarrow{\sigma}{\bf M}\to 0,\quad\quad u=(u_{1},\dotsc,u_{k})\in(\Delta^{\ast})^{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.3" class="ltx_p">and by choosing a section of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> by taking
logarithms of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the canonical
pairing between <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a
well-defined principal polarization on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Over the co-character <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> defined
by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\mapsto(u^{r_{1}},\dots,u^{r_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\mapsto(u^{r_{1}},\dots,u^{r_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the construction
specializes to the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-variable Mumford Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Construction 3.3 (1-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>
associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=r_{1}B_{1}+\cdots+r_{k}B_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=r_{1}B_{1}+\cdots+r_{k}B_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the relevant
fan is the restriction of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the inverse image
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\vec{r}\subset({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\vec{r}\subset({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>; here
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}|_{{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\vec{r}}\simeq{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}|_{{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\vec{r}}\simeq{\rm Cone}({\mathcal{T}})</annotation></semantics></math>
for a tiling <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> depending on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular,
by Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 3.7. ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>,
the monodromy cone
(see Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Definition 2.7. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>)
of the degeneration
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\ast}\colon X^{*}(B_{1},\dots,B_{k})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\ast}\colon X^{*}(B_{1},\dots,B_{k})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is given by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\coloneqq{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\coloneqq{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, we may, as in Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Construction 3.3 (1-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>,
perform the multivariable construction
relatively over the torus
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})/\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle)\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})/\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle)\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
by twisting the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-action by some elements
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=(a_{ij})\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=(a_{ij})\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, we have quotiented by
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so as to reduce redundant
moduli of the general fiber as much as possible.
We denote the resulting fibration
by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}\colon X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\mathcal{S}})\to(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>univ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}\colon X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\mathcal{S}})\to(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}\to({\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})/\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle)\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}\to({\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})/\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle)\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polydisk
bundle with</p>
<table id="S3.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}=g(g+1)/2+k-\dim\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>univ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}=g(g+1)/2+k-\dim\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.3" class="ltx_p">Note that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}\colon X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\mathcal{S}})\to(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>univ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}_{\circ}\colon X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\mathcal{S}})\to(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally trivial
deformation of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, as analytic germs about the zero section
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>univ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{k})^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the universal cover of the former
is the product of the universal cover of the latter
with the torus of
twists <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})/\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle)\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})/\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle)\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\clubsuit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">♣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\clubsuit</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of fans, the (possibly rational)
origin section of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is declared to be the image of the
subtorus whose cocharacter lattice is
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0\}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\subset{\bf N}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0\}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}\subset{\bf N}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\dotsc,B_{k}\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\dotsc,B_{k}\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\ast}\colon X^{*}(B_{1},\dots,B_{k})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\ast}\colon X^{*}(B_{1},\dots,B_{k})\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the family of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional PPAVs defined in
(<a href="#S3.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>),
with fiber <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(\Delta^{\ast})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(\Delta^{\ast})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, as we have seen
above,
the monodromy bilinear form about <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{u_{i}=0\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{u_{i}=0\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> (Def. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Definition 2.6. ‣ 2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>) equals <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
for a suitable symplectic basis of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
cf. Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem30" title="Corollary 2.30. ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.30</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(multiparameter case, cone of a fan for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that the cone</p>
<table id="S3.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\coloneqq{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\cdots,B_{k}\}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\coloneqq{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\cdots,B_{k}\}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is not standard affine, or not even simplicial.
Recall from Remark <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem28" title="Remark 2.28. ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.28</span></a> that
for some étale cover
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have a
toroidal extension
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\hookrightarrow\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\hookrightarrow\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
whose monodromy
cone is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For an appropriate choice of fan <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> gives
a degeneration of abelian varieties over
a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polydisk <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}\subset Y({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}\subset Y({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In fact, we may descend
the construction to an analytic open
neighborhood of the torus fixed point in the affine toric
variety <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
when <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported
rather in the vector space <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
periodic with respect to the same action (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).
Here, the subscript <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
refers to the fact that
we take the toric variety of the polyhedral cone
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, sitting
inside the vector space <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than
inside <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Taking the universal twist,
we produce a degeneration</p>
<table id="S3.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\mathcal{S}})\to T({\mathbb{B}})\subset Y({\mathbb{B}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\rm univ}_{\circ}({\mathcal{S}})\to T({\mathbb{B}})\subset Y({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where now <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the toric
variety of the polyhedral cone
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> is an analytic tubular neighborhood
of the deepest toric boundary stratum.
Then, following Section <a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>,
we may analytically glue <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> along the complement <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>
of the toric boundary to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Taking
the gluing to respect the zero sections, we produce a degeneration</p>
<table id="S3.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X^{\rm univ}_{+}({\mathcal{S}})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\cup_{T^{*}({\mathbb{B}})}T({\mathbb{B}})\eqqcolon\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{+}\subset\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≕</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\rm univ}_{+}({\mathcal{S}})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\cup_{T^{*}({\mathbb{B}})}T({\mathbb{B}})\eqqcolon\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{+}\subset\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which
analytically extends
the universal family
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{X}}}_{g}\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{X}}}_{g}\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One may unify
the set-up of toroidal
extensions of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> laid out in Section
<a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, with the toroidal
construction of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>, by
viewing <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a fan
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math> in
the larger space <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{g}\oplus{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{g}\oplus{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{g}\oplus{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{g}\oplus{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> admitting
a morphism of fans <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}\to{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}\to{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> via projection to the second factor.
Namikawa was the first to introduce
such “mixed cone decompositions”,
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, Sec. 3]</cite> and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, Sec. 9]</cite>
(though in these texts,
the focus is one particular Mumford construction,
similar to Construction <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="Construction 6.5 (Mumford construction of second Voronoi type). ‣ 6.1. The universal family of abelian torsors with theta divisor ‣ 6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The family <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\rm univ}_{+}({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>univ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\rm univ}_{+}({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>
further extends
to a proper, flat family</p>
<table id="S3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{\rm univ}\colon X^{\rm univ}({\mathcal{S}})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{\rm univ}\colon X^{\rm univ}({\mathcal{S}})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.2" class="ltx_p">surjecting onto the base <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, this follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Ch. VI.1]</cite>. Defining <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math> as above, and
taking <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\rm max}({\mathbb{B}})\subset Y({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\rm max}({\mathbb{B}})\subset Y({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>
as the maximal analytic open neighborhood
of the toric boundary over
which the action of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> on the inverse
image of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\rm max}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\rm max}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ})</annotation></semantics></math>
is properly discontinuous, the base <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\rm max}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\rm max}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>
surjects onto the toroidal extension
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\rm max}({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\rm max}({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the relative closure
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathcal{H}}_{g}\subset{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>,
see (<a href="#S2.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), in the toric
variety <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathbb{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathbb{B}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then,
the family descends from a partial
uniformization of toroidal strata
fibering over Baily–Borel strata
of intermediate dimension, to yield (<a href="#S3.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The precise relation to Faltings–Chai
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Ch. VI.1]</cite> is that the fan
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math>
is an instance of a
“<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(X)\ltimes X^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(X)\ltimes X^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>-admissible
polyhedral cone decomposition”
(for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>) in the terminology of
<span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">loc.cit.</span> up to the following
three
minor modifications: We do not
demand as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Ch. VI, Def.1.3.(iii)]</cite>
that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math>
defines
a complete fan
(i.e. a compactification of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>),
when working on an étale cover
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> we only demand
admissibility for a finite index subgroup,
and we do not require the existence of a
polarization function,
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Ch. VI.1, Rem. 1.6.(a)]</cite>.
The constructions used to prove
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Ch. VI.1, Thm. 1.13]</cite> generalize
to this setting, to yield an extension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{X}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathfrak{G}}}\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathfrak{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔊</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔉</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{X}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathfrak{G}}}\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathfrak{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the universal family, for any morphism
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{G}}\to{\mathfrak{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔊</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝔉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{G}}\to{\mathfrak{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> from a mixed cone decomposition
(for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{X}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{X}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>)
to a cone decomposition (for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>).
The family (<a href="#S3.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) is a special case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It is crucial
for our applications in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>
to achieve both a smooth total
space <span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> flatness
over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
(see Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 5.3. ‣ 5.1. Mumford degenerations with nodal singularities ‣ 5. Nodal and semistable morphisms over higher-dimensional bases ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>).
As mentioned in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Ch. VI, Def. 1.3.(v), Rem. 1.4]</cite>,
achieving both of these properties is a
hard combinatorial problem, closely related
to the main result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>; in general,
it is only possible after modifying the base
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As additional
historical notes, the first example of a complete
fan for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> (equidimensional but
not regular) was provided by Namikawa,
see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, Sec. 13, Prop. 13.5, Thm. 13.6]</cite>. The generalization
of Faltings–Chai to the more general
setting of mixed Shimura varieties
is Pink’s dissertation, see especially
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Ex. 2.25, Ch. 6, Ch. 10]</cite>
for discussion relevant to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\clubsuit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">♣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\clubsuit</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As an example of the above construction,
the Tate curve extends the family of elliptic curves
<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}/u^{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}/u^{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> over the unit
disk <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{u}=\{u\in{\mathbb{C}}\,:\,|u|&lt;1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{u}=\{u\in{\mathbb{C}}\,:\,|u|&lt;1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, see Example <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem31" title="Example 3.31. ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.31</span></a>.
The maximally extended base, on which <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>
acts properly discontinuously, is
<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{u}\supset\Delta_{u}^{\ast}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>⊃</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{u}\supset\Delta_{u}^{\ast}\simeq{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The family over
<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{u}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{u}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> descends
(as an orbifold)
along the infinite degree surjection <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathbb{H}}\to\SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}\backslash{\mathbb{H}}\to\SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>, to the universal family over
the orbifold <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{1}=\SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{1}=\SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Only the punctured disc <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{u}(e^{-2\pi})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{u}(e^{-2\pi})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the smaller analytic disk
<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{u}(e^{-2\pi})\coloneqq\{u\in{\mathbb{C}}\,:\,|u|&lt;e^{-2\pi}\}\subset\Delta_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{u}(e^{-2\pi})\coloneqq\{u\in{\mathbb{C}}\,:\,|u|&lt;e^{-2\pi}\}\subset\Delta_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>
embeds, on the level of coarse spaces,
into <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}({\mathbb{Z}})\backslash{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>
(as stacks, the degree
of the map from <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{u}(e^{-2\pi})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{u}(e^{-2\pi})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
onto its image is,
rather, equal to two,
because of the <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/2</annotation></semantics></math>-gerbe on the target).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An additional subtlety is that, to glue
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\rm univ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\rm univ}</annotation></semantics></math> to the universal family, requires
in general, a cover of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
which is étale in the punctured neighborhood
of the boundary, but branched over the boundary divisors.
For instance, when <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}B</annotation></semantics></math> is a ray,
this branched cover is necessary if and only if
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is imprimitive (Rem. <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Remark 3.6. ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>).
Such a branched cover of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is guaranteed to exist
in an affine open neighborhood of the
deepest toroidal stratum, as shown
in Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem29" title="Proposition 2.29. ‣ 2.5. Toroidal extensions ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.29</span></a>.
But it is unclear, in general, whether there
exists a <span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">global</span> étale cover of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> achieving the desired branching
behavior. For example, supposing
the monodromy cone were of the form
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{3B_{1},5B_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{3B_{1},5B_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},B_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},B_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> primitive—is then the local, toroidal
branched cover of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math> which
is branched to orders <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> over the two toric
boundary divisors <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{1},\partial_{2}\subset\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔹</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{1},\partial_{2}\subset\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by
passing to a further finite index
subgroup of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sp</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp_{2g}({\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Regardless, this finite cover does
exist in an
affine open neighborhood of the relevant
boundary stratum. By a slight
abuse of notation, we continue
to notate the resulting cover and
its toroidal extension
by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
even though <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is only a finite étale
over a Zariski open subset of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To summarize Constructions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> and
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fan in
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying the properties described in Constructions
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>.
In particular, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant under the action (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>),
for a collection of symmetric
bilinear forms <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}\subset{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}\subset{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> which are the
rays generating a polyhedral cone in
the space of positive semidefinite bilinear forms on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>,
with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{k}B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{k}B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> positive-definite.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>
produces a flat, proper extension of
the universal family over
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>,
in the category of complex analytic spaces,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\rm univ}({\mathcal{S}})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\rm univ}({\mathcal{S}})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now examine when a Mumford degeneration
is <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-trivial:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.14</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{(1,\dots,1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{(1,\dots,1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a tiling
(as opposed to a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-tiling) of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then the multivariable Mumford construction
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
(see <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>)
is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-trivial: <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{X({\mathcal{S}})}\sim 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{X({\mathcal{S}})}\sim 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The universal cover of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>
admits an analytic open embedding
into the toric variety <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>, whose anticanonical
divisor is the reduced toric boundary. This toric boundary
in turn is the reduced inverse image of the union
of the coordinate hyperplanes
<math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(u_{1}\cdots u_{k})\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{1}\cdots u_{k})\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> under
the toric
morphism <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathcal{S}})\to Y({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k})\simeq{\mathbb{C}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathcal{S}})\to Y({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k})\simeq{\mathbb{C}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, if the inverse image of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(u_{1}\cdots u_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{1}\cdots u_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>
equals its reduced inverse image, we conclude
that <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> and in turn <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> are
relatively <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-trivial.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">To check that the divisors contained in the
inverse image of <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are reduced, it suffices
to restrict to the arc
<math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\mapsto(u,\dots,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\mapsto(u,\dots,u)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now apply Remark <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Remark 3.4. ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Weight filtration and dual complex</h3>

<div id="S3.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.15</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fan
Mumford Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>
and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fiber over a point
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\bf N}\to H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})\to{\bf M}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐍</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\bf N}\to H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})\to{\bf M}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">see (<a href="#S3.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>).
Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}={\bf N}\subset H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐍</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}={\bf N}\subset H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight
filtration <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-2}=W_{-1}\subset W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-2}=W_{-1}\subset W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the limiting mixed Hodge structure on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
That is, there are integral isomorphisms</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\simeq{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\simeq{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\simeq{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\simeq{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}\simeq N_{i}^{\rm mon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mon</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}\simeq N_{i}^{\rm mon}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\simeq B_{i}^{\rm mon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mon</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\simeq B_{i}^{\rm mon}</annotation></semantics></math>
where
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}^{\rm mon}\colon{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})\to{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mon</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}^{\rm mon}\colon{\rm gr}^{W}_{0}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})\to{\rm gr}^{W}_{-2}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{\rm mon}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}}))^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mon</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{\rm mon}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}({\rm gr}^{W}_{0}H_{1}(X_{t},{\mathbb{Z}}))^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>
are the monodromy
operators and bilinear forms of Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. Degenerations of PPAVs ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">The identification of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>
with the stated graded pieces of the weight filtration follows
by construction, see e.g. (<a href="#S3.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>)—the homology group
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}({\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*},{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐍</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}({\bf N}\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*},{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>
is spanned by
the vanishing cycles,
which are null-homologous in the neighborhood
of any <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional toric stratum of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 3.7. ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>
shows that the monodromy operator <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}^{\rm mon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mon</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}^{\rm mon}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees
with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}\colon{\bf M}\to{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}\colon{\bf M}\to{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math> and the hypothesis that
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\sum_{i=1}^{k}B_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\sum_{i=1}^{k}B_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{-2}=({\rm im}\,N)^{\rm sat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>sat</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{-2}=({\rm im}\,N)^{\rm sat}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\sum_{i=1}^{k}N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\sum_{i=1}^{k}N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.16</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fan
Mumford Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>. Then there is a canonical isomorphism
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}\simeq H_{1}(\Gamma(X_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}\simeq H_{1}(\Gamma(X_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(X_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(X_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
is the dual (polyhedral) complex of the central fiber <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The dual polyhedral complex of the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-prequotient
is the infinite periodic polyhedral decomposition
of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the preimage of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,\dots,1)\in{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,\dots,1)\in{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
under the morphism of fans <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}\to({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}\to({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, see
Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>. It follows
that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(X_{0})\simeq{\mathcal{S}}_{(1,\dots,1)}/N({\bf M})={\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(X_{0})\simeq{\mathcal{S}}_{(1,\dots,1)}/N({\bf M})={\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}/\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, there is a canonical
isomorphism <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}(\Gamma(X_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}(\Gamma(X_{0}),{\mathbb{Z}})\simeq{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Mumford construction, polytope version</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We discuss now a polytopal version of
the Mumford degeneration, which outputs
a relatively projective degeneration, together
with a relatively ample line bundle.
Furthermore, it is isomorphic to the fan construction
as in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, for an appropriate
choice of fan <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf N}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our approach is, in part,
inspired by Gross–Siebert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Sec. 2]</cite>,
and their construction of canonical theta functions,
building on the classical
theory of theta functions, see
e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, Prop. II.1.3 and Thm. II.1.3]</cite>.
It is primarily based on a “PL version”
of the classical theory,
in line with Alexeev–Nakamura
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A={\mathbb{C}}^{g}/({\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A={\mathbb{C}}^{g}/({\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be an
abelian variety with principal polarization <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
The classical theory of theta functions
studies explicit sections of the powers of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a
lift of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, by pulling back to
the universal cover <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon{\mathbb{C}}^{g}\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon{\mathbb{C}}^{g}\to A</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}\simeq{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{C}}^{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}\simeq{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{C}}^{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such sections can be understood
via holomorphic functions on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, with appropriate
factors of automorphy under the deck action
of the periods <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\oplus{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Such holomorphic functions are called
<em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">theta functions</em>. We do the same here, for
the intermediate cover <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{g}\to A</annotation></semantics></math> discussed in the introduction, of the fibers <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of a degenerating family
of PPAVs <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}\to(\Delta^{*})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. These theta functions
extend as holomorphic sections of a line
bundle over a toric extension of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the standard torus
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}\coloneqq{\mathbb{R}}^{g}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}={\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}/{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}\coloneqq{\mathbb{R}}^{g}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{g}={\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}/{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L}</annotation></semantics></math> to
be the sheaf on <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-piecewise linear functions
modulo the subsheaf of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear
functions. On an open set
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}/{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}/{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sections
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}(U)\coloneqq\{f\colon U\to{\mathbb{R}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}(U)\coloneqq\{f\colon U\to{\mathbb{R}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
consist of continuous, piecewise linear
functions, which in a domain <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset U</annotation></semantics></math> of linearity
are of the form</p>
<table id="S3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f|_{D}({\bf m})=a_{1}m_{1}+\cdots+a_{g}m_{g}+a_{g+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f|_{D}({\bf m})=a_{1}m_{1}+\cdots+a_{g}m_{g}+a_{g+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are integral coordinates on
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> which define local coordinates on <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sheaf
of locally <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear functions on <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, of the same shape.
A section <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>
can be understood globally on <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> in terms
of its “bending locus” (Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem17" title="Definition 3.17. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.17</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The pull-back of a section
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})</annotation></semantics></math> to
the universal cover <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
lifts to a <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-piecewise linear function
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}({\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}({\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is integer-valued
on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> and, more generally, <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{w}{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{w}{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued
on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{w}{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{w}{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> for any positive integer <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The function <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto b_{i}(x+{\bf m})-b_{i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto b_{i}(x+{\bf m})-b_{i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a linear function on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf m}\in{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf m}\in{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is lifted from <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Conversely, any
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-piecewise linear function <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\colon M_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\colon M_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}(x+{\bf m})-b_{i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}(x+{\bf m})-b_{i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is linear,
for all <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf m}\in{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf m}\in{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>,
descends to a section
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})</annotation></semantics></math> that determines
the equivalence class <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> uniquely
(where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\sim b_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\sim b_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if the difference
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}-b_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}-b_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is linear).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The domains
of linearity of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are rational polyhedra.
The sections <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for which a lifted
function <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex
form a convex polyhedral subcone
of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.17</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighted
polyhedral complex in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, called the
<span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bending locus</span> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Its faces are the codimension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>
polytopes in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> along which
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-linear,
and the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bending parameter</span>
(positive when <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex)
defining the weight on
a codimension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> polytope,
is the change in slope of
the restriction of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
an integral, complementary segment
to the hyperplane containing the face.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.18</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})</annotation></semantics></math>
be a collection of convex sections, with
nontrivial bending in every direction, i.e. for every non-zero
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf m}\in{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf m}\in{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists some
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math> for which
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}(x+{\bf m})-b_{i}(x)\not\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≢</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}(x+{\bf m})-b_{i}(x)\not\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math> is not
identically zero.
Equivalently,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> cuts
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> into polytopes.
We say that the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
are <span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dicing</span> if the polyhedral
decomposition
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
has integral
vertices.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The dicing condition is quite restrictive, since
only the origin of <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> may appear as a vertex
of <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. We will
relax this hypothesis in Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem38" title="Construction 3.38. ‣ 3.6. Base change and Veronese embedding ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.38</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.19</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}={\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}={\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}/{\bf M}={\mathbb{R}}/{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}/{\bf M}={\mathbb{R}}/{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define a PL function
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\colon{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\colon{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is linear on each interval
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[m,m+1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[m,m+1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and has values on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> equal to
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(m)=\tfrac{1}{2}(m^{2}-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(m)=\tfrac{1}{2}(m^{2}-m)</annotation></semantics></math>. The graph of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is depicted
in the left of Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Example 3.31. ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The locus where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>
is non-linear is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Bend}(\overline{b})=\left\{0\right\}\in{\mathbb{R}}/{\mathbb{Z}}={\mathbb{T}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Bend}(\overline{b})=\left\{0\right\}\in{\mathbb{R}}/{\mathbb{Z}}={\mathbb{T}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with weight one,
see Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.20</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any projective morphism <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of
analytic spaces gives rise to an algebraic family
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{X}}\to\Spec{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dotsc,u_{k}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{X}}\to\Spec{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dotsc,u_{k}]]</annotation></semantics></math>,
the <em id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">formal completion</em> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, the projectivity of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that
there is a positive integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\Delta^{k}\times\mathbb{P}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\Delta^{k}\times\mathbb{P}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is cut out
by homogeneous polynomials
whose coefficients are convergent power series.
The completion <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then cut out by the
same equations, viewing the convergent
power series as formal power series in
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dotsc,u_{k}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dotsc,u_{k}]]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 3.21</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})</annotation></semantics></math>
be a collection of convex sections, with
nontrivial bending in every direction,
and assume that the
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{1},\dotsc,\overline{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{1},\dotsc,\overline{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are dicing.
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{v}\in\tfrac{1}{w}{\bf M}/{\bf M}\in{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{v}\in\tfrac{1}{w}{\bf M}/{\bf M}\in{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
be a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{w}</annotation></semantics></math>-integral point of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some
positive integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> theta function
associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span> to be</p>
<table id="S3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta_{\overline{v}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{g},u_{1},\dots,u_{k})\coloneqq\sum_{v\,\in\,\overline{v}+{\bf M}}(z_{1}^{x_{1}(v)}\cdots z_{g}^{x_{g}(v)}u_{1}^{b_{1}(v)}\cdots u_{k}^{b_{k}(v)})^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">∈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>w</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta_{\overline{v}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{g},u_{1},\dots,u_{k})\coloneqq\sum_{v\,\in\,\overline{v}+{\bf M}}(z_{1}^{x_{1}(v)}\cdots z_{g}^{x_{g}(v)}u_{1}^{b_{1}(v)}\cdots u_{k}^{b_{k}(v)})^{w}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate function,
cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Sec. 4.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The condition that the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> have bending
in every direction ensures that this power series
converges in an appropriate power series ring
(which notably involves both negative and positive
powers of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>). Consider the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]</annotation></semantics></math>-module</p>
<table id="S3.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\coloneqq\bigoplus_{w=0}^{\infty}\,\bigoplus_{\overline{v}\,\in\,\frac{1}{w}{\bf M}/{\bf M}}{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]\cdot\Theta_{\overline{v}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\coloneqq\bigoplus_{w=0}^{\infty}\,\bigoplus_{\overline{v}\,\in\,\frac{1}{w}{\bf M}/{\bf M}}{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]\cdot\Theta_{\overline{v}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Expanding the product of two theta functions
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\overline{v}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\overline{v}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\overline{v}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\overline{v}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of weights <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
by collecting all monomial terms into <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits
(see (<a href="#S3.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) below),
we see that there is an
expansion</p>
<table id="S3.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta_{\overline{v}_{1}}\Theta_{\overline{v}_{2}}=\sum_{\overline{v}_{3}\in\frac{1}{w_{1}+w_{2}}{\bf M}/{\bf M}}c_{\overline{v}_{1}\overline{v}_{2}}^{\overline{v}_{3}}(u_{1},\dots,u_{k})\Theta_{\overline{v}_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta_{\overline{v}_{1}}\Theta_{\overline{v}_{2}}=\sum_{\overline{v}_{3}\in\frac{1}{w_{1}+w_{2}}{\bf M}/{\bf M}}c_{\overline{v}_{1}\overline{v}_{2}}^{\overline{v}_{3}}(u_{1},\dots,u_{k})\Theta_{\overline{v}_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the coefficients
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\overline{v}_{1}\overline{v}_{2}}^{\overline{v}_{3}}(u_{1},\dots,u_{k})\in{\mathbb{Z}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\overline{v}_{1}\overline{v}_{2}}^{\overline{v}_{3}}(u_{1},\dots,u_{k})\in{\mathbb{Z}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]</annotation></semantics></math> are
integral power series, as opposed to simply Laurent series,
by the convexity of the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Eqn. (2.5)]</cite>.
Note that
to get a nonzero coefficient,
there must be a lift of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{v}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{v}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of the form</p>
<table id="S3.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{3}=\frac{w_{1}v_{1}+w_{2}v_{2}}{w_{1}+w_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{3}=\frac{w_{1}v_{1}+w_{2}v_{2}}{w_{1}+w_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Hence
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> is
closed under multiplication. It is, furthermore,
a finitely generated, graded ring over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the resulting projective
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme</p>
<table id="S3.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm Proj}_{{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]}\,R(\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k})\to{\rm Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm Proj}_{{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]}\,R(\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k})\to{\rm Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.5" class="ltx_p">It is a degeneration of PPAVs of dimension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>,
with the theta functions providing the projective
embedding,
which is the
completion (in the sense of Remark <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem20" title="Remark 3.20. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.20</span></a>)
of a relatively projective
complex analytic degeneration</p>
<table id="S3.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f\colon X({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f\colon X({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.6" class="ltx_p">over a polydisk. This
can be proven e.g. by observing
that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\overline{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\overline{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> are analytically convergent
power series on a Mumford fan construction
when all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|u_{i}|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|u_{i}|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, a fact which
is justified in the course of the proof
of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem29" title="Theorem 3.29. ‣ 3.4. Comparison of polytope and fan constructions ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.29</span></a>.
We call <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mumford degeneration</span>
associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.22</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">That the generic fiber of (<a href="#S3.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) is
an abelian variety also
follows from the classical theory of theta functions.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We have assumed that the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are dicing,
see Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem18" title="Definition 3.18. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.18</span></a>.
Define <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
as the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">overgraph</span> of the collection of functions
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(23)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Gamma=\Gamma(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})+({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Gamma=\Gamma(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})+({\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0})^{k}\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Then, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is an
infinite convex, locally finite
polytope in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
whose faces are integral polytopes.
We may think of the lattice
points, in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\cap({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\cap({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, as
the monomial sections of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>
on the corresponding infinite type
toric variety <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=Y_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=Y_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>,
see Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Toric varieties ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.
Similarly, we may think of the
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{w}</annotation></semantics></math>-integral points of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> as the
monomial sections of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math>,
cf. Remark <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem21" title="Remark 2.21. ‣ 2.4. Toric varieties ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.21</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then,
the theta function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\overline{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\overline{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{v}\in\tfrac{1}{w}{\bf M}/{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{v}\in\tfrac{1}{w}{\bf M}/{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> is the result
of summing such monomials, over an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit,
where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf m}\in{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf m}\in{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by
the affine-linear action</p>
<table id="S3.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(24)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(x,\vec{r})\mapsto(x+{\bf m},\,\vec{r}+\vec{b}(x+{\bf m})-\vec{b}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(x,\vec{r})\mapsto(x+{\bf m},\,\vec{r}+\vec{b}(x+{\bf m})-\vec{b}(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{b}=(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{b}=(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that this action of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Form the normal fan <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
As (<a href="#S3.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) gives an action
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, it induces an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-action
on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This action agrees with the
action (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>)
for a multivariable Mumford fan construction,
associated to the bilinear forms <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\in{\rm Sym}^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined by the equations</p>
<table id="S3.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(25)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B_{i}({\bf m},{\bf m}^{\prime})\coloneqq b_{i}({\bf m}+{\bf m}^{\prime})-b_{i}({\bf m})-b_{i}({\bf m}^{\prime})+b_{i}(0),\quad{\bf m},{\bf m}^{\prime}\in{\bf M}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B_{i}({\bf m},{\bf m}^{\prime})\coloneqq b_{i}({\bf m}+{\bf m}^{\prime})-b_{i}({\bf m})-b_{i}({\bf m}^{\prime})+b_{i}(0),\quad{\bf m},{\bf m}^{\prime}\in{\bf M}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6.1" class="ltx_p">By the convexity of the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the normal fan
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}\subset({\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}\subset({\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>
admits a canonical morphism to the fan
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{k}\simeq({\mathbb{R}}^{k})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>≃</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{k}\simeq({\mathbb{R}}^{k})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>,
given by restricting
linear functionals in
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the data of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with
the projection to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, defines the data
of a Mumford fan Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>.
We will prove that
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k})\simeq X({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k})\simeq X({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>
in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem29" title="Theorem 3.29. ‣ 3.4. Comparison of polytope and fan constructions ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.29</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To “twist” the construction, as
in Constructions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Construction 3.3 (1-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>,
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>, by
some continuous parameters
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=(a_{ij})\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=(a_{ij})\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, and produce
a universal degeneration which
represents all possible continuous moduli of degenerations
of the specified combinatorial type, we must introduce
appropriate coefficients</p>
<table id="S3.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta_{\overline{v}}^{a}(z_{1},\dots,z_{g},u_{1},\dots,u_{k})\coloneqq\sum_{v\,\in\,\overline{v}+{\bf M}}d_{v}(a)(z_{1}^{x_{1}(v)}\cdots z_{g}^{x_{g}(v)}u_{1}^{b_{1}(v)}\cdots u_{k}^{b_{k}(v)})^{w}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">∈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>w</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\overline{v}}^{a}(z_{1},\dots,z_{g},u_{1},\dots,u_{k})\coloneqq\sum_{v\,\in\,\overline{v}+{\bf M}}d_{v}(a)(z_{1}^{x_{1}(v)}\cdots z_{g}^{x_{g}(v)}u_{1}^{b_{1}(v)}\cdots u_{k}^{b_{k}(v)})^{w}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}(a)\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}(a)\in{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. This twists the structure
constants to give a graded ring
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{a}({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{a}({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>
and ranging over the moduli of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>,
produces a relatively projective
multivariable Mumford degeneration over the base
which is a
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Spec}\,{\mathbb{C}}[[u_{1},\dots,u_{k}]]</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle
over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}^{*})^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\dim{\mathcal{A}}_{g}-{\rm rank}\,{\mathbb{R}}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\dim{\mathcal{A}}_{g}-{\rm rank}\,{\mathbb{R}}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It agrees on the general fiber
with the quotient by the family of subgroups
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="au_{1}^{B_{1}}\cdots u_{k}^{B_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">au_{1}^{B_{1}}\cdots u_{k}^{B_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These constants <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> form part of the
so-called “degeneration data” of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
 <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\clubsuit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">♣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\clubsuit</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_notation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation 3.23</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The construction
of the ring <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>
depends only on the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and not the lifts
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to PL functions on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. But
it is usually easiest to specify
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by providing the PL function
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
With this in mind, we will henceforth
notate the Mumford degeneration
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Bend}(b_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Bend}(b_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.24</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The number of theta functions
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\overline{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\overline{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>
is exactly <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. These functions form the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">theta basis</span>,
a canonical (up to scaling) basis of sections of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(X({b_{1},\dots,b_{k}}),\,{\mathcal{L}}^{\otimes w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(X({b_{1},\dots,b_{k}}),\,{\mathcal{L}}^{\otimes w})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a lift
of the relative principal
polarization. In particular,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta=V(\Theta_{\overline{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta=V(\Theta_{\overline{0}})</annotation></semantics></math> extends as a
Cartier divisor over the degenerating family
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({b_{1},\dots,b_{k}})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({b_{1},\dots,b_{k}})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are dicing,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer polyhedron,
which is why <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is Cartier on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=Y_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=Y_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It also admits a natural linearization with respect
to the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-action. This is why the principal
polarization extends, as a line bundle,
to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Absent the dicing condition,
one may consider the least positive
integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> for which the overgraph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>
is a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{d}({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{d}({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-integral polyhedron.
Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> defines an integral polyhedron
and so descends as a line bundle on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>
which is a lift of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> times a principal
polarization on the smooth fibers.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.25</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b_{1},\dots,b_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b_{1},\dots,b_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
are <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-dicing</span> if
they are <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-piecewise linear,
the corresponding
overgraph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{d}({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{d}({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-integral
polyhedron,
and in the local form (<a href="#S3.E18" title="Equation 18 ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>),
the slopes <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\dots,a_{g}\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\dots,a_{g}\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> are still integral, but
we allow <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{g+1}\in\tfrac{1}{d}{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{g+1}\in\tfrac{1}{d}{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote sheaves
of functions with such a local form by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{d}{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{d}{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{d}{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{d}{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 3.26</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Like Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>
vis-à-vis Construction
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>, we
generalize Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a> to the case
where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
are the extremal rays of a convex polyhedral cone
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{b}\subset H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕓</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{b}\subset H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})</annotation></semantics></math> mapping isomorphically to a convex polyhedral cone
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> under the map
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}_{i}\mapsto B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}_{i}\mapsto B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in\Sym^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in\Sym^{2}{\bf M}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.E25" title="Equation 25 ‣ Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>).
We replace <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> by the PL function</p>
<table id="S3.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\to({\mathbb{R}}\mathbbm{b})^{\vee}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}^{\dim{\mathbb{R}}\mathbbm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕓</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕓</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\to({\mathbb{R}}\mathbbm{b})^{\vee}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}^{\dim{\mathbb{R}}\mathbbm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex61"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bf m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bf m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex61.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto(b\mapsto b({\bf m}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto(b\mapsto b({\bf m})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Otherwise, the
details of Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>
are the same.
The output is a relatively projective
degeneration of abelian varieties</p>
<table id="S3.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(\mathbbm{b})\to T(\mathbbm{b})\subset Y(\mathbbm{b})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(\mathbbm{b})\to T(\mathbbm{b})\subset Y(\mathbbm{b})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.3" class="ltx_p">over an analytic tubular neighborhood
of the torus fixed point of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(\mathbbm{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\mathbbm{b})</annotation></semantics></math>. Performing
this construction with the universal twist by
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})/\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in{\rm Sym}_{g\times g}({\mathbb{Z}})/\langle{\mathbb{B}}\rangle\otimes{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,
for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}={\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\{B_{1},\dots,B_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and extending/descending over the toroidal
extension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as in
Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>, we may
produce an analytically-locally relatively projective,
analytic
extension of the universal family</p>
<table id="S3.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\rm univ}(\mathbbm{b})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\rm univ}(\mathbbm{b})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.4" class="ltx_p">see Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 3.13. ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>
(we descend from an open set
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\rm max}(\mathbbm{b})\subset Y(\mathbbm{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\rm max}(\mathbbm{b})\subset Y(\mathbbm{b})</annotation></semantics></math>
as in Construction <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Construction 3.11 (multiparameter case, cone of a fan for 𝒜_𝑔). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a> to verify
the analytic-local projectivity).
We will show in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. The second Voronoi fan and Alexeev’s theorem ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\rm univ}(\mathbbm{b})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>univ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\rm univ}(\mathbbm{b})\to\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is an étale-locally projective morphism
of algebraic spaces over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathcal{A}}}_{g}^{\,{\mathbb{B}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\clubsuit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">♣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\clubsuit</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.27</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary case of interest in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> is where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{b}</annotation></semantics></math>
defines a simplicial cone <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}\subset{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>
and in this setting, Constructions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>
and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a> over a polydisk will suffice.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The next proposition follows directly from Construction
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.28</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{g},b_{1},\dots,b_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{g},b_{1},\dots,b_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>
define a polarized, multivariable Mumford degeneration
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the following hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The intersection complex
of the fiber </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{I}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> over the generic point
of the coordinate subspace </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(u_{i}\,:\,i\in I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(u_{i}\,:\,i\in I)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is
the polyhedral decomposition
</span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i\in I}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i\in I}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
of </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The polytopes of this
polyhedral decomposition, when compact,
are the polytopes of the polarized toric components,
in the sense of Remark </span><a href="#S2.Thmtheorem21" title="Remark 2.21. ‣ 2.4. Toric varieties ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.21</span></a><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Non-compact faces </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of
</span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i\in I}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i\in I}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
are of the form </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\simeq F_{0}\times{\mathbb{T}}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\simeq F_{0}\times{\mathbb{T}}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> compact.
The dimension of the abelian part
of the corresponding component
of </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{I}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. This component is a toric variety
bundle over an abelian </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-fold, possibly self-glued,
where the toric variety has polytope </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The universal cover of the Mumford construction
is the toric variety <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> whose polytope is <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>,
and hence <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the intersection complex
of this universal cover. Then the intersection complex
of the Mumford construction itself is the
quotient by the <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math> action, and the stated description
follows—components with an abelian factor
of dimension <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
arise from infinite
faces of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>
stabilized by a rank <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> subgroup of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
See also Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem29" title="Theorem 3.29. ‣ 3.4. Comparison of polytope and fan constructions ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.29</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Comparison of polytope and fan constructions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explain why the polytope construction
of the Mumford degeneration coincides with the fan
construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.29</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}\}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})</annotation></semantics></math>
be a collection of convex sections, with
nontrivial bending in every direction, which are dicing.
Define <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as in
(<a href="#S3.E23" title="Equation 23 ‣ Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>), and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the normal
fan to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there is a canonical isomorphism of analytic spaces</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\simeq X(\mathcal{S})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\simeq X(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\overline{b}_{1},\dots,\overline{b}_{k}})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the polytope Mumford degeneration defined
in (<a href="#S3.E22" title="Equation 22 ‣ Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) and where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(\mathcal{S})\to\Delta^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(\mathcal{S})\to\Delta^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the fan Mumford degeneration defined in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Construction 3.8 (𝑘-parameter case). ‣ 3.1. Mumford construction, fan version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=Y_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=Y_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> be the locally finite
type toric variety defined by the
polytope <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, as in Section
<a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Toric varieties ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>. Then <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=Y({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=Y({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>
by definition, and the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-action
(<a href="#S3.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) on <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, which we denote
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bf m}\cdot-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf m}\cdot-</annotation></semantics></math>, defines a linearization of the
line bundle <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> associated to
the polytope <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{w}</annotation></semantics></math>-integral point
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,\vec{r})\in\Gamma\cap\tfrac{1}{w}({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,\vec{r})\in\Gamma\cap\tfrac{1}{w}({\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>
defines an analytic section
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z,u)^{w(v,\vec{r})}\coloneqq z^{wv}u^{w\vec{r}}\in H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z,u)^{w(v,\vec{r})}\coloneqq z^{wv}u^{w\vec{r}}\in H^{0}(Y,{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(w))</annotation></semantics></math>.
When this <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{w}</annotation></semantics></math>-integral
point lies on the graph <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have the equality</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(26)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta_{\overline{v}}(z,u)=\sum_{{\bf m}\in{\bf M}}(z,u)^{w({\bf m}\cdot(v,\vec{r}))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta_{\overline{v}}(z,u)=\sum_{{\bf m}\in{\bf M}}(z,u)^{w({\bf m}\cdot(v,\vec{r}))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">of analytic
functions, on the analytic open subset of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
where all <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|u_{i}|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|u_{i}|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Convergence
holds because the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> having nontrivial
bending in all directions (see Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem18" title="Definition 3.18. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.18</span></a>),
and so with respect to an exhaustion
of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the powers of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> grow quadratically
while the powers of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> only grow linearly.
If, rather,
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,\vec{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,\vec{r})</annotation></semantics></math> lies above the graph <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(b_{1},\dots,b_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>,
the corresponding sum over the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit
is simply a monomial in <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>
times <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\overline{v}}(z,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\overline{v}}(z,u)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We deduce that <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\overline{v}}(z,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\overline{v}}(z,u)</annotation></semantics></math>
descends, as an analytic section, to
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(X({\mathcal{S}}),{\mathcal{L}}^{\otimes w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(X({\mathcal{S}}),{\mathcal{L}}^{\otimes w})</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the descent of the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-linearized line
bundle <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}</annotation></semantics></math>-quotient <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices then to verify
that these descended sections define,
for some fixed <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, a relatively very ample
line bundle on <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>—in particular,
that they separate points and tangents.
The argument is essentially the same as
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Thm. 4.7]</cite>, replacing
the Delaunay decomposition with the
more general decompositions
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
that we consider.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">In fact, in the setting where all polytopal faces <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
cut by <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>Bend</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i}{\rm Bend}(\overline{b}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
are embedded as opposed to immersed in <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the multiplication rule (<a href="#S3.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Construction 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Mumford construction, polytope version ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) for
theta functions <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\overline{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\overline{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{v}\in F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{v}\in F</annotation></semantics></math>, reduce, modulo the ideal
<math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{1},\dots,u_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{1},\dots,u_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, to the usual multiplication
rule (Rem. <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem21" title="Remark 2.21. ‣ 2.4. Toric varieties ‣ 2. Preliminary material ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.21</span></a>)
for the monomial sections of the powers of
the line bundle <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}|_{Y_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}|_{Y_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the toric
stratum <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{F}\subset X({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{F}\subset X({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>;
see Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem33" title="Lemma 3.33. ‣ Example 3.32 (Multiplication of theta functions). ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.33</span></a>.
Assuming <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, we also ensure
that the non-normal union <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}({\mathcal{S}})=\lim_{F}Y_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mi>F</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}({\mathcal{S}})=\lim_{F}Y_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>
is projectively
embedded via <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}^{\otimes w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}^{\otimes w}</annotation></semantics></math>. One deduces very ampleness
for all fibers, by the openness of very ampleness.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For instance, we have the following special
case, for <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-parameter degenerations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.30</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PL</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}\in H^{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{g},{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm PL}/{\mathbb{Z}}{\rm L})</annotation></semantics></math>
be dicing, with PL lift <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\colon{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> and define
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\coloneqq\Gamma(b)+{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\coloneqq\Gamma(b)+{\mathbb{R}}_{\geq 0}\subset{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}\times{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the normal fan
to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, and define <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in{\rm{Sym}^{2}}{\bf M}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in{\rm{Sym}^{2}}{\bf M}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B({\bf m},{\bf m}^{\prime})\coloneqq b({\bf m}+{\bf m}^{\prime})-b({\bf m})-b({\bf m}^{\prime})+b(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B({\bf m},{\bf m}^{\prime})\coloneqq b({\bf m}+{\bf m}^{\prime})-b({\bf m})-b({\bf m}^{\prime})+b(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant tiling <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\bf N}}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\bf N}}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> by slicing <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> at height <math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\rm{Cone}}(\mathcal{T}))\simeq X(\mathcal{S})\simeq X(\overline{b})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\rm{Cone}}(\mathcal{T}))\simeq X(\mathcal{S})\simeq X(\overline{b}).</annotation></semantics></math>  <span id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">∎</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Examples</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now discuss some examples
of the Mumford construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.31</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The basic example is the Tate curve
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{*}/u^{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{*}/u^{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}/{\bf M}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}/{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf M}_{\mathbb{R}}/{\bf M}\simeq{\mathbb{R}}/{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define a PL function
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\colon{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\colon{\mathbb{R}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is linear on each interval
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[m,m+1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[m,m+1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and has values on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> equal to
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(m)=\tfrac{1}{2}(m^{2}-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(m)=\tfrac{1}{2}(m^{2}-m)</annotation></semantics></math>. The graph of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is depicted
in the left of Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Example 3.31. ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2507.15695v3/tate1.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:248/225;" width="248" height="225" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2507.15695v3/tate2.png" id="S3.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:225/225;" width="225" height="225" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Mumford polytope construction of the Tate curve,
<math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{0/1}(z,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{0/1}(z,u)</annotation></semantics></math> in blue, <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{0/2}(z,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{0/2}(z,u)</annotation></semantics></math> in red, <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{1/2}(z,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{1/2}(z,u)</annotation></semantics></math> in green.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the shaded region in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Example 3.31. ‣ 3.5. Examples ‣ 3. The Mumford construction ‣ Combinatorics and Hodge theory of degenerations of abelian varieties: A survey of the Mumford construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and the convex hull of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(m,\tfrac{1}{2}(m^{2}-m)):m\in{\bf M}\}\subset{\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}={\mathbb{Z}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(m,\tfrac{1}{2}(m^{2}-m)):m\in{\bf M}\}\subset{\bf M}\times{\mathbb{Z}}={\mathbb{Z}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The theta functions of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> are</p>
<table id="S3.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

